#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Cross-demo copy check (K-623, K-607, K-624, K-640).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The eight demos are eight standalone Rails apps, so shared
# application code is not shared at all — it is HAND-COPIED.
# `script/equihash_register.rb` ships in seven demos,
# `app/operations/wire_arguments.rb` in three, and nine
# db/migrate files in up to seven. Nothing checked that the copies still agree.
#
# They drift, and the drift is invisible, and it need not be a comment:
# `script/equihash_register.rb` is the proof — a constant rename reached three
# of its five copies (057196e) and stopped there, unnoticed, until this check
# was written. A set of copies that happens to agree today is a STATE, not a
# GUARD: the next edit that lands in one copy and not the others is exactly as
# undetectable.
#
# So this is a CHECK, not a convention: it derives every relative path that
# exists in two or more demos, and fails when a copy disagrees with its siblings
# in a way nobody declared. "Someone must remember to propagate the edit" becomes
# "CI notices when they did not". Same shape, and the same reason, as
# bin/check-ci-tasks (K-619) — this is a SIBLING rather than a new mode of that
# script because the two answer different questions from DIFFERENT INPUTS: that
# one compares ci.yml against each demo's rake tasks, this one compares demo
# files against each other, and bin/check-demo-derivations (K-1051) compares one
# demo's narrative document against THAT SAME demo's own producers.
#
# THE INPUT IS THE BOUNDARY, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE (K-1096). That sentence used
# to end «folding them together would put two unrelated invariants behind one
# exit code and one --write contract», and the reason does not survive its own
# file. MEASURED from the `# ── … ──` banners below: this script now enforces
# NINETEEN independent invariants behind exactly one exit code and one --write
# contract — seventeen when that sentence was written, plus T-120's fragment
# rule and K-1262's partial-convergence rule. Read as a rule about COUNT, the
# sentence argued for splitting this file nineteen ways. It was never about count.
#
# THAT NINETEEN IS A COUNT OF BANNERS AND IT MISSES ONE, re-derived 2026-08-31
# while writing the `--self-test` below, whose coverage table is generated from
# the same banners and therefore had to ask the question: the K-962
# lockfile-PLATFORMS parity rule is written with a `# -- … --` banner rather than
# a `# ── … ──` one, so the derivation cannot see it. It is an independent
# invariant on the same corpus by every test the others meet. The sentence above
# is left as written because a ledger row cites it.
#
# AND THE CORRECTION IS NOT A NEW CARDINAL, BECAUSE THAT IS THE DEFECT THIS FILE
# EXISTS TO PREVENT (K-1340). The correction that used to stand here announced an
# «honest total» of its own, as a typed cardinal, and was overtaken within days:
# the file then carried TWO wrong counts of itself plus a note explaining why one
# of them was wrong — in the guard whose whole subject is inventories that drift
# because somebody keeps them by hand.
#
# THE COUNT IS DERIVED, AND THE DERIVATION IS THE ANSWER: run
#
#     ./bin/check-demo-copies --self-test --list
#
# whose FIRST line reports how many arms cover how many bannered invariants plus
# the unbannered ones, from this file's own banners rather than from a list — so
# a rule added below reads as UNCOVERED rather than as absent, and a number that
# has moved cannot go stale in a paragraph. Do not copy that number back up here.
#
# They all share the manifest, the discovery pass and the normalisation, so one
# script per invariant would re-declare the demo corpus once per invariant —
# which is precisely the drift this file exists to stop. What a sibling may not
# share is its INPUT: a script that compares two different pairs of things cannot tell a
# reader which pair disagreed, and no amount of output formatting fixes that,
# whereas a second invariant over the SAME corpus costs nothing but a section.
#
# The cost of holding them all behind one exit code is real and is not argued
# away here: CI cannot distinguish a failing prose claim from a failing
# migration pair without reading the output, and `--write` can rewrite copies
# for a reason the operator did not intend. If that becomes the thing that
# hurts, the split is a T- with a PENDING: clause — a behavioural change to a
# CI gate — and not a header edit.
#
# THE RULE IT ENFORCES. Every relative path found in >= 2 demos must appear in
# the COPIES manifest below with a rule and a reason:
#
#   :identical — byte-for-byte in every demo that ships it.
#   :code      — identical once comments and whitespace are normalised away, so
#                each copy's header prose may name its own demo. Ripper does the
#                stripping, so a `#` inside a string or regex is not a comment.
#                MAGIC comments are the exception and are compared (K-628).
#   :per_demo  — a genuine per-demo implementation that shares only a filename.
#                The FILE is not compared; the manifest entry records WHY they
#                differ, so the next reader does not have to diff seven files to
#                find out. A path here may still have individual METHODS and
#                CONSTANTS held in lockstep — see the T-120 fragment rule below,
#                which exists because «not compared» was letting shared units
#                grow inside these files with nothing watching them. And since
#                K-1262 the claim is CHECKED rather than taken: any SUBSET of the
#                copies that agrees once comments and whitespace are normalised
#                away must be declared in the entry's `agree:` map, as
#                `:lockstep` (one artefact, hand-copied — and then held) or
#                `:coincidence` (unrelated reasons, capped). Three of
#                `script/schema_flow.rb`'s seven copies were one file at 37
#                normalised lines under an entry that said each asserted its own
#                demo's verbs, and nothing at either granularity could see it:
#                the fragment rule's tripwire needed UNANIMITY, so two of three
#                converging fired nothing, and the file level had no tripwire.
#
# `ships:` is the OTHER declaration a copy set can carry, and it answers a
# question `except:` cannot: WHICH demos are supposed to have the file at all.
# The lockstep rule compares whatever copies it finds, so a demo that stops
# shipping one — or starts — changes the set silently. K-860 and K-883 are why
# it exists: skooti's `script/bound_assistant.rb` sat in the tree with ZERO
# consumers in its own demo, held byte-identical to three copies that were
# live, and the answer was to delete it. Deleting alone would have left nothing to stop the
# next wave copying them back, because a re-added byte-identical copy is exactly
# what the lockstep rule is happy with. So an entry may name its set exactly:
# a demo that ships the file and is not listed FAILS, and a listed demo that
# does not ship it FAILS too. Entries without `ships:` are unconstrained — the
# declaration is for sets somebody has deliberately reasoned about.
#
# `except:` is the escape hatch for one copy that legitimately differs from its
# siblings (stylish's stub_user_idp is a DB-backed staff IdP). It is a demo => reason map,
# the same shape as `ungated:` in ci.yml's demos matrix, and it is checked too:
# an exception for a demo that no longer ships the file, or whose copy has since
# converged on its siblings, is an error rather than a comfortable lie.
#
# A path that turns up in a second demo with no manifest entry fails the build.
# That is the property that matters: a NEW copy cannot appear silently, which is
# how six of these got here.
#
# ONE AXIS THE LOCKSTEP RULE CANNOT SEE (K-640). The db/migrate entries keep the
# demos' generator-output migrations identical to EACH OTHER — but those files
# are not hand-written: `rails g kiosk:install` emits them from kiosk-server's
# templates, and all seven demos can drift from the ENGINE together and stay
# green, because they only ever agree with each other. So every install-generator
# template is also declared, in GENERATOR_TEMPLATES below, rendered with the
# generator's own default options (the values every demo was generated with),
# and the render must byte-match every demo copy — regenerating the engine's
# schema without regenerating the demos now fails this check.
#
# AND ONE MORE (K-648). Both rules need a shared handle to grab a file by: the
# lockstep rule matches copies by relative path, the template rule by the
# basename a `.tt` emits. A HAND-WRITTEN migration that ships in two demos at
# demo-specific timestamps has neither — every copy is a singleton path and no
# template renders it — so the pair was compared to NOTHING. Those basenames
# are declared in MIGRATION_PAIRS below and compared across all their copies,
# and an undeclared one fails the build, same as an undeclared path.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not an argument that copying is right. Every entry
# below is an interim state made honest, and an entry is DELETED the day its
# extraction lands rather than kept as a memorial — which is what happened to
# `app/models/uuid_check.rb` and `app/services/pow_difficulty.rb` under T-189,
# each of them promoted into the gem that owns the contract.
#
# MAGIC COMMENTS ARE THE ONE COMMENT `:code` STILL COMPARES (K-628). Ripper drops
# every comment, magic ones included, so until this was added a copy that lost
# `# frozen_string_literal: true` compared equal to one that had it — and three of
# the four application_controller.rb copies had lost it, unnoticed, because a
# magic comment is the one comment that changes how the file RUNS. So the leading
# magic-comment block is extracted and compared separately, on the recognised
# Ruby set only (see MAGIC_COMMENTS): prose stays ignored, `# frozen_string_literal`
# does not. `:identical` needs nothing here — byte equality already covers it.
#
# Stdlib only (digest + ripper + erb), no bundle, no database. MEASURED at head
# 2026-08-31: ~1.0 s on a warm checkout, of which about two thirds is the T-117
# citation rule building its ~700-file constant index. It was ~0.2 s — the same
# profile as bin/check-ci-tasks — before that rule existed, and the cost is
# recorded here rather than in a commit message because the next rule that wants
# to walk a tree should know what the last one spent.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-demo-copies          # check
#   bin/check-demo-copies --list   # print the manifests as tables
#   bin/check-demo-copies --self-test         # plant a break per invariant in a
#                                             # throwaway copy and prove each reddens
#   bin/check-demo-copies --self-test --list  # the coverage table, run nothing

require "digest"
require "erb"
require "open3"
require "ripper"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# ── --self-test: one planted break per invariant, in a throwaway copy ────────
#
# WHY THIS FILE HAS ONE, WHICH IS NOT «EVERY GUARD SHOULD». That principle is
# explicitly rejected in this workspace — most scripts should not have one — and
# this section does not lean on it. Two facts about THIS script are the whole
# argument (K-1267):
#
#   * IT CARRIES MANY INDEPENDENT INVARIANTS BEHIND ONE EXIT CODE — `--list`
#     below derives how many, and the number is deliberately not typed here
#     (K-1340) — and a bare
#     run answers ONE question about all of them at once: is the tree clean today.
#     It cannot answer the other one — does arm N still go RED on the break it was
#     written for. At that many, a single reverted regex takes one
#     invariant out of service while the exit code stays 0, which is not a
#     hypothesis: K-1240 measured exactly that, one repository over, where a bare
#     run stayed green while `--self-test` went red on 22 arms.
#   * THE PROOF THAT EACH ARM REDDENS WAS BEING THROWN AWAY. Landing K-1262 and
#     K-1263 took NINE hand-driven plant-and-restore cycles across five demo files
#     and this script. Every one was a fixture somebody wrote, watched go red, and
#     deleted; not one was reproducible by the next session. Each arm below is one
#     of those nine, or a sibling of one, kept.
#
# The five VACUITY guards this file already runs on every invocation answer a
# DIFFERENT question and are not replaced here: «does this rule still have a live
# subject in the tree in front of you». No fixture can answer that, and no vacuity
# guard can answer «does the rule still fail on a violation».
#
# HOW IT RUNS. `git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard` in ROOT, copied
# into throwaway trees under a mktmpdir; one is checked green as a BASELINE; then
# each arm plants its break in a tree, runs the COPY of this script from that
# tree's own `bin/`, asserts exit 1 AND asserts the output names the invariant,
# then restores each touched file from the pristine original and re-checksums it
# against ROOT. The re-checksum is not ceremony: a plant is only put back if the
# bytes match the original again, and «restored» and «restored to something else»
# are indistinguishable without the digest. Nothing under ROOT is ever written by
# any of this — the plant lands in the throwaway copy. A plant whose
# needle no longer occurs is an arm FAILURE, never a pass: a needle that has moved
# is exactly how an arm stops testing anything while still reading green.
#
# TWO KINDS OF ARM, declared per row, because they answer different questions and
# one of them is easy to get backwards:
#
#   :break   — a FAITHFUL MISTAKE, the thing a real session does: a one-sided
#              edit, a copy appearing where no `ships:` set expects it, an
#              exception that has gone stale, a claim a sweep dropped from one
#              document. Four rows plant in the MANIFEST instead of the tree,
#              because that is where those mistakes are made. None of them is a
#              syntax error a parser would catch.
#   :vacuity — the rule's own MECHANISM switched off in the copy of this script,
#              to prove the in-band vacuity guard watching that mechanism is
#              itself alive. NOTE THE POLARITY, which is the trap T-129 hit and
#              K-1262 got right: for a rule that is a BAN — «no undeclared group
#              of agreeing copies» — «the pattern must match something» is
#              backwards, because a ban whose violations match nothing is the ban
#              being obeyed. So these arms switch off the MEASUREMENT and expect
#              the declaration-liveness guard to fire, never a violation count.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, because an over-claimed self-test is worse than none:
#
#   * It proves each listed arm reddens on ONE break. It does not enumerate the
#     breaks an invariant could miss, and a rule with an arm here is not correct
#     because of it.
#   * The `:vacuity` arms prove the GUARD fires when its counter reads zero. They
#     do NOT prove the counter tracks what its name says.
#   * Most rules carry an in-band vacuity guard and only some of those guards
#     have an arm here; neither count is typed, for K-1340's reason — `--list`
#     marks a vacuity arm `V` and a break arm `B`, so both are read off the
#     table. `--self-test --list` prints the coverage
#     table — which rules have an arm, of which kind — derived from this file's
#     own `# ── … ──` banners rather than from a list, so a rule added later reads
#     as UNCOVERED rather than as absent.
#   * The fixture is a copy of the WORKING tree, so an arm is only as meaningful
#     as the baseline: a red bare run makes this red too, by design, and says so
#     before it runs anything else.
#
# COST, measured 2026-08-31 on this laptop: ~0.2 s to build one tree, ~0.8 s per
# guard run, four trees in parallel.
if ARGV.include?("--self-test")
  require "fileutils"
  require "open3"
  require "rbconfig"
  require "tmpdir"

  SELF_TEST_PROBE_RB =
    "# frozen_string_literal: true\n\n# A shared helper two demos grew in the same wave.\n" \
    "class SelfTestProbe\n  def self.call = :ok\nend\n"

  # The K-1088 and K-1090 arms plant in ALL SEVEN copies at once, and that is the
  # point rather than a shortcut: both rows record seven copies held identical and
  # identically WRONG, so a one-sided plant would be caught by the lockstep rule
  # and would prove nothing about the ban.
  SELF_TEST_SEVEN = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling philslist skooti stylish tudu].freeze
  SELF_TEST_AC_PLANT = SELF_TEST_SEVEN.map do |d|
    [:edit, "kiosk-demo-#{d}/app/controllers/application_controller.rb",
     "endpoints, the catalog link and the modules this origin serves",
     "endpoints and the schema, query, run, pay endpoints"]
  end.freeze
  SELF_TEST_404_PLANT = SELF_TEST_SEVEN.map do |d|
    [:edit, "kiosk-demo-#{d}/public/404.html",
     "for the register/login endpoints, the catalog link and the modules this",
     "for the register/login, schema, query, run, pay endpoints and the modules this"]
  end.freeze

  # The whole FRAGMENT_EXCLUDED entry for one path, removed the way a session that
  # thought the note was stale would remove it.
  SELF_TEST_EXCLUSION_RE = /^  "script\/schema_flow\.rb" =>\n(?:^ {4}".*\n)+/
  SELF_TEST_DSL_LINE     = "    dsl_names.each { |name| fragment_dsl_ids[\"\#{rel}\#\#{name}\"] = true }\n"

  # Banners that are DATA or a HELPER rather than an invariant. The coverage table
  # is `every banner in this file` MINUS this list, so a rule added later appears
  # as UNCOVERED instead of not appearing at all, and an arm naming a banner that
  # no longer exists is an error rather than a silent no-op.
  SELF_TEST_NOT_A_RULE = [
    "--self-test: one planted break per invariant, in a throwaway copy",
    "The manifest",
    "What is NOT compared, and why (K-629)",
    "The engine side of the generator output (K-640, hardened at K-646)",
    "WHY THERE IS NO WAY TO RECORD AN EXCEPTION ANY MORE",
    "Cross-demo singleton pairs (K-648)",
    "Normalisation",
    "Magic comments (K-628)",
    "Discovery",
    "The e2e harness's fixtures, paired with the demo files they came from",
  ].freeze

  # The one rule with no `# ── … ──` banner, declared so the check below can tell
  # «an arm for an unbannered rule» from «an arm for a banner that has moved».
  # It is the twentieth invariant; see the note on the header's count.
  SELF_TEST_UNBANNERED = ["every demo lockfile resolves for the SAME platforms (K-962)"].freeze

  # THE PLANT TABLE. `rule:` is the banner the arm aims at, verbatim, so coverage
  # is derived from this column. `ops:` are applied in order to one fixture tree
  # and undone from the pristine original afterwards; `expect:` must appear in the
  # planted run's output, and the run must exit 1.
  SELF_TEST_PLANTS = [
    { id: "code-copy-diverges", kind: :break,
      rule: "The one comparison this whole file exists for",
      what: "a rename reaches ONE of seven `:code` copies — 057196e's exact shape",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-philslist/script/equihash_register.rb", "equihash_solve", "solve_equihash"]],
      expect: /script\/equihash_register\.rb: declared :code — the copies must agree.+\[philslist\]/ },
    { id: "magic-comment-lost", kind: :break,
      rule: "The one comparison this whole file exists for",
      what: "one copy loses `# frozen_string_literal: true` — K-628's exact shape",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-stylish/app/controllers/application_controller.rb",
              "# frozen_string_literal: true\n", ""]],
      expect: /application_controller\.rb: declared :code — prose may differ per demo, MAGIC comments may not/ },
    { id: "ships-stray-copy", kind: :break,
      rule: "The one comparison this whole file exists for",
      what: "a copy reappears in a demo outside the entry's `ships:` set — K-860/K-883 undone",
      ops:  [[:copy, "kiosk-demo-atablefor/script/bound_assistant.rb",
              "kiosk-demo-hoteling/script/bound_assistant.rb"]],
      expect: /script\/bound_assistant\.rb: hoteling ships this file but is not in its declared `ships:` set/ },
    { id: "ships-copy-gone", kind: :break,
      rule: "The one comparison this whole file exists for",
      what: "a demo named in `ships:` stops shipping the file and the declaration is not moved",
      ops:  [[:delete, "kiosk-demo-stylish/script/bound_assistant.rb"]],
      expect: /script\/bound_assistant\.rb: `ships:` names stylish, which no longer ships/ },
    { id: "except-has-converged", kind: :break,
      rule: "The one comparison this whole file exists for",
      what: "the excepted copy converges on its siblings and the exception is left standing",
      ops:  [[:copy, "kiosk-demo-tudu/config/environments/test.rb",
              "kiosk-demo-prove/config/environments/test.rb"]],
      expect: /config\/environments\/test\.rb: `except:` for prove is stale/ },

    { id: "new-copy-undeclared", kind: :break,
      rule: "Undeclared and stale manifest entries",
      what: "a helper lands in two demos at once with no manifest entry — how six of these got here",
      ops:  [[:write, "kiosk-demo-tudu/app/services/self_test_probe.rb", SELF_TEST_PROBE_RB],
             [:write, "kiosk-demo-philslist/app/services/self_test_probe.rb", SELF_TEST_PROBE_RB]],
      expect: /app\/services\/self_test_probe\.rb: exists in 2 demos \(philslist, tudu\) but is not declared/ },
    { id: "entry-no-longer-duplicated", kind: :break,
      rule: "Undeclared and stale manifest entries",
      what: "a copy is deleted, leaving an entry that compares one file to itself",
      ops:  [[:delete, "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/services/bad_proof_counter.rb"]],
      expect: /app\/services\/bad_proof_counter\.rb: declared in bin\/check-demo-copies but only atablefor ships it/ },

    { id: "exclusion-no-longer-true", kind: :break,
      rule: "The recorded exclusion must stay true (K-629)",
      what: "a cleanup deletes an inert generator file from all but one demo and leaves the record",
      ops:  [[:delete, "kiosk-demo-philslist/config/initializers/cors.rb"],
             [:delete, "kiosk-demo-stylish/config/initializers/cors.rb"]],
      expect: /config\/initializers\/cors\.rb: recorded in SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED .+ but only 1 demo ships it/ },

    { id: "template-regenerated-alone", kind: :break,
      rule: "Template ↔ copy: the drift the lockstep rule cannot see (K-640)",
      what: "the engine's install template is edited and the demos are not regenerated",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-server/lib/generators/kiosk/install/templates/create_kiosk_schema.rb.tt",
              "  def up\n", "  def up\n    # engine-side change that never reached the demos\n"]],
      expect: /create_kiosk_schema\.rb\.tt: rendered with the generator's default options/ },
    { id: "template-undeclared", kind: :break,
      rule: "Template ↔ copy: the drift the lockstep rule cannot see (K-640)",
      what: "a new install-generator template appears with no GENERATOR_TEMPLATES entry",
      ops:  [[:write, "kiosk-server/lib/generators/kiosk/install/templates/create_kiosk_audit_log.rb.tt",
              "class CreateKioskAuditLog < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.0]\n  def change; end\nend\n"]],
      expect: /create_kiosk_audit_log\.rb\.tt: new install-generator template not declared/ },

    { id: "ledger-id-in-generator-template", kind: :break,
      rule: "NO LEDGER ID IN A SHIPPED GENERATOR TEMPLATE (K-1298)",
      what: "a fix wave cites its own row in a template `kiosk:install` copies into a consumer's repo",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-server/lib/generators/kiosk/install/templates/initializer.rb.tt",
              "  # Column on `users` that holds the principal id. Default :id.",
              "  # Column on `users` that holds the principal id. Default :id (K-830)."]],
      expect: /initializer\.rb\.tt:\d+: a ledger id in a template the install generator WRITES INTO A/ },

    { id: "ledger-id-in-demo-code", kind: :break,
      rule: "NO LEDGER ID IN CODE A DEMO RUNS (K-1383)",
      what: "an id lands in a `desc` body — what `rake -D` prints, K-1323's exact operator-facing shape",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-hoteling/lib/tasks/demo.rake",
              "DB-free unit spec for the WireArguments shape guards",
              "DB-free unit spec for the WireArguments shape guards (K-1099)"]],
      expect: /lib\/tasks\/demo\.rake:\d+: a ledger id OUTSIDE a comment, in code this demo RUNS/ },
    { id: "vacuity-demo-ledger-ids", kind: :vacuity,
      rule: "NO LEDGER ID IN CODE A DEMO RUNS (K-1383)",
      what: "the pattern stops matching a shape people write, so a BAN with a dead pattern must say so",
      ops:  [[:edit, "bin/check-demo-copies",
              "  \"# The past-slot filter, K-480.\",\n", "  \"# The past-slot filter, 480.\",\n"]],
      expect: /the K-1383 ledger-id pattern no longer matches its own fixture/ },
    { id: "vacuity-demo-ledger-lexer", kind: :vacuity,
      rule: "NO LEDGER ID IN CODE A DEMO RUNS (K-1383)",
      what: "the comment-versus-code distinction the whole ban rests on must be proved, not assumed",
      ops:  [[:edit, "bin/check-demo-copies",
              "DEMO_ID_IN_COMMENT = \"# The past-slot filter, K-480.\\n\"\n",
              "DEMO_ID_IN_COMMENT = %q{MSG = \"K-480\"} + \"\n\"\n"]],
      expect: /Ripper no longer separates an id in a COMMENT from an id in a STRING/ },

    { id: "migration-pair-drift", kind: :break,
      rule: "Cross-demo singleton pairs: the migrations neither rule above sees (K-648)",
      what: "the hand-written migration that ships at demo-specific timestamps is edited in one demo",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-atablefor/db/migrate/20260721000001_add_devise_columns_to_users.rb",
              "add_index :users, :email, unique: true",
              "add_index :users, :email, unique: true, where: \"email IS NOT NULL\""]],
      expect: /db\/migrate\/\*_add_devise_columns_to_users\.rb: declared :code — the copies must agree/ },

    { id: "autoload-once-line-gone", kind: :break,
      rule: "The app/services autoload-once declaration (K-502, re-anchored by T-189)",
      what: "the autoload_once_paths line K-502 made load-bearing is dropped from one demo",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-skooti/config/application.rb", /^.*autoload_once_paths.*\n/, ""]],
      expect: /skooti: config\/initializers\/kiosk\.rb names .* from app\/services but config\/application\.rb does not put app\/services/ },

    { id: "operation-result-regrows", kind: :break,
      rule: "What is LEFT in a per-demo operation_result.rb (K-792)",
