      what: "a demo ships no compose file, so the one command its README promises does not exist",
      ops:  [[:delete, "kiosk-demo-tudu/compose.yaml"]],
      expect: /kiosk-demo-tudu ships no compose file/ },
    { id: "compose-runs-another-demo", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "a copied-and-half-edited compose file brings up a DIFFERENT demo under this demo's name — " \
            "the shape that is invisible because the image carries all eight",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-tudu/compose.yaml",
              "working_dir: /kiosk/kiosk-demo-tudu", "working_dir: /kiosk/kiosk-demo-skooti"]],
      expect: %r{kiosk-demo-tudu/compose\.yaml: its `working_dir` is not} },
    { id: "compose-port-drifts-from-the-task", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "a demo moves the port its own tasks serve on and the compose file keeps publishing the old one",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-stylish/lib/tasks/demo.rake",
              'ENV.fetch("PORT", "3005")', 'ENV.fetch("PORT", "3105")']],
      expect: %r{kiosk-demo-stylish/compose\.yaml: it does not serve on port 3105} },
    { id: "compose-shared-half-drifts", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "one compose file's database service is edited and the seven siblings are not — the " \
            "hand-copy drift this whole script exists for, in the newest copied file",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-philslist/compose.yaml",
              "pg_isready -U postgres", "pg_isready -U postgres -d postgres"]],
      expect: %r{compose\.yaml: the compose file differs from the \d+ other\(s\)} },
    { id: "compose-port-collision", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "two demos publish the same host port, so they cannot be up at once — and comparing two " \
            "storefronts side by side is how an operator reads the wire",
      ops:  [[:edit, "kiosk-demo-tudu/compose.yaml",
              '      - "3007:3007"', "      - \"3007:3007\"\n      - \"3003:3007\""]],
      expect: /all publish host port 3003/ },
    { id: "second-image-recipe", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "a demo grows its own Dockerfile, which is eight copies of an install list and cannot " \
            "build anyway — the context has to be the repository",
      ops:  [[:write, "kiosk-demo-skooti/Dockerfile", "FROM ruby:4.0.1-slim\nRUN echo hi\n"]],
      expect: %r{kiosk-demo-skooti/Dockerfile is a SECOND container image recipe} },
    { id: "image-ruby-drifts-from-ci", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "the image's Ruby stops being one this repository's CI runs, so the path an operator " \
            "meets FIRST is the one interpreter nothing here is tested on",
      ops:  [[:edit, "Dockerfile", /^ARG RUBY_VERSION=[0-9][0-9.]*$/, "ARG RUBY_VERSION=3.2.9"]],
      expect: /`ARG RUBY_VERSION=3\.2\.9` names a Ruby \.github\/workflows\/ci\.yml never runs/ },
    { id: "orphan-container-ignore", kind: :break,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "a demo grows back the `.dockerignore` two earlier passes deleted one demo at a time — " \
            "and the repository now HAS a root recipe, so an ancestor test would call it legitimate",
      ops:  [[:write, "kiosk-demo-hoteling/.dockerignore",
              "# See https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#dockerignore-file\n/.git/\n/tmp/*\n"]],
      expect: /kiosk-demo-hoteling\/\.dockerignore is an ORPHAN/ },
    { id: "vacuity-container-detector", kind: :vacuity,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "the ignore-file pattern stops matching its own subject — the zero that reads like a clean tree",
      ops:  [[:edit, "bin/check-demo-copies",
              "CONTAINER_IGNORE_RE = /\\A\\.(?:docker|container|podman)ignore\\z/",
              "CONTAINER_IGNORE_RE = /\\A\\.nothing-matches-this\\z/"]],
      expect: /the container-tooling detector reads "\.dockerignore" as neither/ },
    { id: "vacuity-container-corpus", kind: :vacuity,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "the file walk comes back empty by EITHER route, so every container file in the tree is invisible",
      ops:  [[:edit, "bin/check-demo-copies",
              "container_tracked = container_git.success? ? container_ls.split(\"\\0\").reject(&:empty?) : container_walk(ROOT)",
              "container_tracked = []"]],
      expect: /the container-tooling rule walked 0 (?:tracked|working-tree) path\(s\)/ },
    { id: "vacuity-no-port-anywhere", kind: :vacuity,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "no demo declares a PORT default any more, so the compose-port rule compares nothing and " \
            "reads as coverage. STRUCTURAL: the op walks every demo rake file rather than naming the " \
            "seven that have one today, so a demo added tomorrow drains with the rest",
      ops:  [[:each, "kiosk-demo-*/lib/tasks/demo.rake",
              /ENV\.fetch\("PORT",\s*"\d+"\)/, 'ENV.fetch("PORT")']],
      expect: /not ONE demo yielded a port default from its own/ },
    { id: "vacuity-ci-postgres-unreadable", kind: :vacuity,
      rule: "EVERY DEMO SHIPS A CONTAINER PATH, AND THE RECIPE IS SHARED (T-192, K-1335)",
      what: "ci.yml stops declaring a Postgres service, so the database-major rule compares the " \
            "compose files against nothing and any major at all reads as clean",
      ops:  [[:edit, ".github/workflows/ci.yml", "image: postgres:17", "image: pg-service:17"]],
      expect: /no `image: postgres:<major>` in \.github\/workflows\/ci\.yml/ },
  ].freeze

  # ── coverage, derived from this file's own banners ────────────────────────
  st_banners    = File.readlines(__FILE__).filter_map { |l| l[/\A# ── (.+?)\s*─*\s*\z/, 1] }
  st_invariants = st_banners - SELF_TEST_NOT_A_RULE
  st_aimed      = SELF_TEST_PLANTS.map { |p| p[:rule] }.uniq
  st_orphans    = st_aimed - st_invariants - SELF_TEST_UNBANNERED
  st_uncovered  = st_invariants - st_aimed

  st_bad   = []
  st_notes = []

  unless st_orphans.empty?
    st_bad << "#{st_orphans.length} arm(s) name a `rule:` that is neither a banner in this file nor a " \
              "declared unbannered rule: #{st_orphans.map(&:inspect).join(', ')}. A banner was reworded " \
              "and the plant table was not — which is how a coverage table starts describing a file " \
              "that no longer exists."
  end
  if (SELF_TEST_NOT_A_RULE - st_banners).any?
    st_bad << "SELF_TEST_NOT_A_RULE names #{(SELF_TEST_NOT_A_RULE - st_banners).map(&:inspect).join(', ')}, " \
              "which is not a banner in this file any more. Update the exclusion list in the same commit " \
              "that moved the banner."
  end

  if ARGV.include?("--list")
    puts "check-demo-copies --self-test --list: #{SELF_TEST_PLANTS.length} arm(s) over " \
         "#{st_invariants.length} bannered invariant(s) + #{SELF_TEST_UNBANNERED.length} unbannered"
    (st_invariants + SELF_TEST_UNBANNERED).each do |rule|
      arms = SELF_TEST_PLANTS.select { |p| p[:rule] == rule }
      if arms.empty?
        puts format("  %-6s %s", "NONE", rule)
      else
        puts format("  %-6s %s", arms.map { |a| a[:kind] == :vacuity ? "V" : "B" }.join, rule)
        arms.each { |a| puts format("         %-32s %s — %s", a[:id], a[:kind], a[:what]) }
      end
    end
    st_bad.each { |b| warn "  ✗ #{b}" }
    exit(st_bad.empty? ? 0 : 1)
  end

  # ── the fixture trees ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  st_ls, st_git = Open3.capture2("git", "-C", ROOT, "ls-files", "-z", "--cached", "--others",
                                 "--exclude-standard")
  unless st_git.success?
    warn "check-demo-copies --self-test: FAIL — `git ls-files` failed in #{ROOT}. The fixture tree is a " \
         "copy of what git carries plus what it does not ignore, and there is nothing to copy without it."
    exit 1
  end
  st_rels = st_ls.split("\0").select { |r| File.file?(File.join(ROOT, r)) }

  st_jobs = [4, SELF_TEST_PLANTS.length].min
  st_t0   = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)

  st_results = Array.new(SELF_TEST_PLANTS.length)

  Dir.mktmpdir("check-demo-copies-self-test") do |tmp|
    st_trees = (0...st_jobs).map do |i|
      tree = File.join(tmp, "tree#{i}")
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(st_rels.map { |r| File.dirname(File.join(tree, r)) }.uniq)
      st_rels.each { |r| FileUtils.copy_file(File.join(ROOT, r), File.join(tree, r), true) }
      tree
    end

    st_run = lambda do |tree|
      out, status = Open3.capture2e(RbConfig.ruby, File.join(tree, "bin", "check-demo-copies"))
      [out, status.exitstatus]
    end

    # SELF-NON-VACUITY, three ways, before any arm means anything.
    mine   = Digest::SHA256.file(__FILE__).hexdigest
    theirs = Digest::SHA256.file(File.join(st_trees[0], "bin/check-demo-copies")).hexdigest
    st_bad << "the fixture's copy of this script is not byte-identical to the one running (#{mine[0, 12]} " \
              "vs #{theirs[0, 12]}), so every arm below tests a different file" unless mine == theirs

    real_demos = Dir[File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-*")].count { |d| File.directory?(d) }
    fix_demos  = Dir[File.join(st_trees[0], "kiosk-demo-*")].count { |d| File.directory?(d) }
    st_bad << "the fixture holds #{fix_demos} demo(s) and #{ROOT} holds #{real_demos} — the copy is not " \
              "the corpus this guard walks" unless real_demos == fix_demos && real_demos.positive?

    base_out, base_code = st_run.call(st_trees[0])
    if base_code.zero?
      puts format("  %-6s %s", "GREEN", "baseline: the untouched copy exits 0, so every arm below is ONE " \
                                        "planted break away from red")
    else
      st_bad << "the BASELINE copy is not green (exit #{base_code.inspect}). No arm below means anything " \
                "until it is — and the usual cause is that a bare run of this guard is red too:\n" +
                base_out.lines.grep(/✗/).first(4).map { |l| "      #{l.rstrip[0, 160]}" }.join("\n")
    end

    if st_bad.empty?
      st_queue = SELF_TEST_PLANTS.each_with_index.to_a
      st_lock  = Mutex.new

      st_trees.map do |tree|
        Thread.new do
          loop do
            plant, idx = st_lock.synchronize { st_queue.shift }
            break if plant.nil?

            saved   = {}
            trouble = nil
            # `:each` names a GLOB rather than one path — it is how a vacuity arm
            # drains the whole corpus without listing the files that are in it
            # today. Expanding it here is what keeps the save/restore/verify
            # machinery below identical for every op kind.
            st_rels = lambda do |op|
              case op[0]
              when :copy then [op[2]]
              when :each then Dir[File.join(tree, op[1])].sort.map { |f| f.delete_prefix("#{tree}/") }
              else [op[1]]
              end
            end
            plant[:ops].each do |op|
              st_rels.call(op).each do |rel|
              saved[rel] = nil unless saved.key?(rel)
              path = File.join(tree, rel)
              # Content AND mode: the `:chmod` arm plants a mode, and a restore that
              # only rewrote bytes would leave the fixture tree permanently altered.
              saved[rel] ||= File.exist?(path) ? [File.binread(path), File.stat(path).mode & 0o7777] : nil
              end
            end

            out, code = nil, nil
            begin
              plant[:ops].each do |op|
                rel  = op[0] == :copy ? op[2] : op[1]
                path = File.join(tree, rel)
                case op[0]
                when :edit
                  before = File.read(path)
                  after  = before.gsub(op[2]) { op[3] }
                  trouble ||= "the needle #{op[2].inspect} no longer occurs in #{rel}" if after == before
                  File.write(path, after)
                when :prepend
                  File.write(path, op[2] + File.read(path))
                when :write
                  FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(path))
                  File.write(path, op[2])
                when :chmod
                  trouble ||= "#{rel} is absent, so there is nothing to chmod" unless File.exist?(path)
                  File.chmod(op[2], path) if File.exist?(path)
                when :delete
                  trouble ||= "#{rel} is already absent, so the deletion plants nothing" unless File.exist?(path)
                  FileUtils.rm_f(path)
                when :copy
                  src = File.join(tree, op[1])
                  trouble ||= "#{op[1]} does not exist, so there is nothing to copy" unless File.exist?(src)
                  FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(path))
                  FileUtils.copy_file(src, path, true) if File.exist?(src)
                when :each
                  # STRUCTURAL: every file the glob finds, none of them named here.
                  hits = Dir[File.join(tree, op[1])].sort
                  trouble ||= "the glob #{op[1].inspect} matches no file, so this arm plants nothing" if hits.empty?
                  touched = 0
                  hits.each do |f|
                    before = File.read(f)
                    after  = before.gsub(op[2]) { op[3] }
                    next if after == before

                    touched += 1
                    File.write(f, after)
                  end
                  trouble ||= "the needle #{op[2].inspect} occurs in none of the #{hits.length} " \
                              "file(s) #{op[1].inspect} matches" if touched.zero?
                end
              end
              out, code = st_run.call(tree) if trouble.nil?
            ensure
              saved.each do |rel, snap|
                p = File.join(tree, rel)
                if snap.nil?
                  FileUtils.rm_f(p)
                else
                  File.binwrite(p, snap[0])
                  File.chmod(snap[1], p)
                end
              end
            end

            # RESTORE VERIFIED, not assumed: every touched file is re-checksummed
            # against the pristine original under ROOT, because a restore that put
            # back different bytes reads exactly like one that worked.
            drift = saved.keys.reject do |rel|
              a = File.join(tree, rel)
              b = File.join(ROOT, rel)
              next false unless File.exist?(a) == File.exist?(b)
              next true unless File.exist?(a)

              Digest::SHA256.file(a).hexdigest == Digest::SHA256.file(b).hexdigest
            end

            st_results[idx] = { plant: plant, trouble: trouble, code: code, out: out.to_s, drift: drift }
          end
        end
      end.each(&:join)
    end
  end

  st_elapsed = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - st_t0

  st_results.compact.each do |r|
    plant = r[:plant]
    label = plant[:kind] == :vacuity ? "VAC" : "RED"
    if r[:trouble]
      st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: the plant did not apply — #{r[:trouble]}. An arm whose break no longer " \
                "lands is an arm that tests nothing while reading green; re-point it at the live text."
      next
    end
    if r[:code] != 1
      st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: planted «#{plant[:what]}» and the guard exited #{r[:code].inspect} " \
                "instead of 1. The invariant «#{plant[:rule]}» no longer catches it."
      next
    end
    unless r[:out] =~ plant[:expect]
      st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: the guard went red as it should, but nothing in its output matched " \
                "#{plant[:expect].inspect} — so it failed for a different reason and this arm proves " \
                "nothing about «#{plant[:rule]}». First failure line:\n      " +
                (r[:out].lines.grep(/✗/).first.to_s.rstrip[0, 200])
      next
    end
    unless r[:drift].empty?
      st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: after restoring, #{r[:drift].join(', ')} no longer checksum-matches the " \
                "original under #{ROOT}. The fixture tree is throwaway so nothing is lost, but a restore " \
                "that does not restore is a failure this arm exists to make visible."
      next
    end
    puts format("  %-6s %-32s %s", label, plant[:id], plant[:what])
  end

  st_notes << "#{st_uncovered.length} of #{st_invariants.length} bannered invariant(s) have NO arm: " \
              "#{st_uncovered.map(&:inspect).join('; ')}" unless st_uncovered.empty?

  if st_bad.empty?
    puts
    puts "check-demo-copies --self-test: OK — #{SELF_TEST_PLANTS.length} arm(s) " \
         "(#{SELF_TEST_PLANTS.count { |p| p[:kind] == :break }} planted breaks, " \
         "#{SELF_TEST_PLANTS.count { |p| p[:kind] == :vacuity }} vacuity), every one RED and naming its " \
         "invariant, over #{st_aimed.length} of #{st_invariants.length + SELF_TEST_UNBANNERED.length} " \
         "invariants, in #{format('%.1f', st_elapsed)} s over #{st_jobs} throwaway tree(s)."
    st_notes.each { |n| puts "note: #{n}" }
    puts "(This proves each listed arm still reddens on ONE break. It does not prove the guard is " \
         "correct, and it is not a substitute for running it bare.)"
    exit 0
  end

  $stdout.flush
  warn "\ncheck-demo-copies --self-test FAILED:\n"
  st_bad.each { |b| warn "  ✗ #{b}" }
  warn "\nAn arm that stops reddening is an invariant that has quietly left service while the bare run " \
       "still exits 0 — the shape K-1240 measured. Repair the rule, or record why the arm is wrong; do " \
       "not delete the arm to make this green."
  exit 1
end

# Where hand-written demo code lives. Deliberately NOT the Rails skeleton
# (bin/, config/, public/, Rakefile, config.ru): those are generator output that
# each demo edits for its own port, host and initializer, and comparing them
# would drown the signal this check exists for. `lib/tasks/*.rake` is outside
# these roots too: a demo's rake file is that storefront's own tasks, and what
# CI runs of them is bin/check-ci-tasks's business. That used to be stated as
# "per-demo by construction, and already covered by bin/check-ci-tasks", and
# K-1043 made both halves false at once (K-1046): the flow-runner helper it
# added is byte-identical in six rake files, and check-ci-tasks compares the
# task INVENTORY, never one demo's rake file against another's. So the file
# stays uncompared and the CONSTRUCT is declared — see the flow-runner rule
# below, which is where any future lockstep fragment in lib/tasks/ belongs.
# What that leaves out, and why, is recorded in SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED below.
SCAN_ROOTS = ["lib", "app", "script", "db/migrate"].freeze

# Named one by one because they are the exceptions: paths outside SCAN_ROOTS
# (or outside `*.rb`) that ARE compared. A path earns a place here either when
# its copies have no per-demo dimension at all (`.gitignore`, the T-048
# statics), or when the single copy that legitimately differs is carried by the
# entry's `except:` map — the six skeleton paths where prove, the API-only
# broker generated on its own, is the sole outlier (K-643).
#
# THE THIRD REASON, and it is the one `spec/` arrives under (T-137): a path can
# be here simply because SCAN_ROOTS cannot see the directory it lives in, so the
# manifest is the ONLY thing that knows the copies exist. `spec/` is not a scan
# root and three demos now ship `spec/wire_arguments_spec.rb`; without this line
# the repo's own rule — «a NEW duplicate fails the build until it is declared» —
# would have been silently untrue for it, which is the same shape as a `:per_demo`
# file whose shared methods nothing compares (T-120).
EXTRA_SCANNED = [
  ".gitignore",
  "spec/wire_arguments_spec.rb",
  "public/robots.txt",
  "public/favicon.ico",
  "public/404.html",
  "public/422.html",
  "public/500.html",
  "config/puma.rb",
  "config/environments/test.rb",
  "config/environments/production.rb",
  "bin/setup",
  "bin/dev",
].freeze

# The one reason six `except:` entries below share (K-643): prove is not a
# divergent COPY of the operator skeleton, it is a different, smaller API-only
# Rails app generated on its own — the recurring shape described atop
# SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED. Stated once so six copies of the sentence cannot
# drift apart.
# The two demos that carry a KYC surface, and the one reason all four of their
# KYC files declare `ships:` (K-657). Stated once so four copies of it cannot
# drift apart, the same way PROVE_SKELETON is.
# The seven OPERATOR demos, by short name — the set every `ships:` that means
# "all of them but the KYC broker" spells. Declared HERE, above COPIES, because
# entries in that manifest need it (bin/setup, bin/dev) as well as the K-1069
# corpus far below; NON_OPERATOR_DEMOS names the other side of the same split.
OPERATOR_SEVEN = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling philslist skooti stylish tudu].freeze

KYC_DEMOS = %w[getgrocery skooti].freeze
KYC_SHIPS =
  "`ships:` pins the set to getgrocery and skooti deliberately (K-657). Phil has DECIDED that " \
  "`request_kyc`/`kyc_status` do not belong among an operator's business-logic verbs — " \
  "`request_kyc` becomes part of the WIRE and the poll query should not exist at all " \
  "(KYC-WIRE-DIRECTION, 2026-08-12) — and the wire that replaces them is T-062, still unbuilt " \
  "because its design depends on operator-sent events (T-046). So the shape is frozen where it " \
  "was reviewed: a THIRD demo growing a copy would spread a surface already decided against, " \
  "and a copy DISAPPEARING is the T-062 migration itself, which must move this declaration in " \
  "the same wave rather than let it rot."

# The two pairs of `app/operations/operation_result.rb` copies that AGREE, and
# the reason each pair does (K-1262). Written once because the same two groups
# are declared twice — on the file entry, whose copies agree whole, and on the
# `#STATUSES` unit entry inside it — and two copies of a reason drift.
OPERATION_RESULT_AGREE = {
  %w[getgrocery skooti] => {
    rule: :coincidence,
    why:  "THE COINCIDENCE THIS ENTRY HAS ARGUED SINCE K-792, NOW DECLARED INSTEAD OF ONLY " \
          "DESCRIBED. Both maps carry the same codes — through `kyc_required` and " \
          "`quota_exceeded` and, since the broker-absence refusals, `action_failed` and " \
          "`module_not_served` — and they carry them for unrelated reasons: getgrocery gates " \
          "alcohol, skooti gates a motorcycle licence, and each arrived at its own set by asking " \
          "which refusals ITS verbs can make. The last two are the closest thing to a shared " \
          "cause the pair has ever had, and they are still not one: both demos call the SAME " \
          "hand-copied broker client, which is itself held `:code`, so the maps agreeing is a " \
          "consequence of that copy rather than of a decision taken twice. `:coincidence` rather " \
          "than `:lockstep` precisely because «even the one match today would be a coincidence to " \
          "enshrine»: holding these two in lockstep would make a legitimate divergence — one " \
          "origin adding a refusal the other cannot make — read as drift. What IS held is the " \
          "membership: a THIRD demo landing on the same set is not an accident twice over, and " \
          "the pair ceasing to agree drops this group in the same commit.",
  },
  %w[philslist tudu] => {
    rule: :coincidence,
    why:  "THE SHORTEST MAPS IN THE FLEET, AT THE SAME TWO CODES, FOR DIFFERENT REASONS. philslist " \
          "lists `bad_request` and `forbidden` and deliberately has NO `not_found`, so a listing " \
          "that does not exist and one that belongs to somebody else answer the same 403 and " \
          "cross-owner probing cannot enumerate ids; tudu lists the same two because a to-do list " \
          "its owner can always see has no third refusal to make. Same pair of codes, two unrelated " \
          "arguments — the same shape as the getgrocery/skooti pair above and declared the same way.",
  },
}.freeze

PROVE_SKELETON =
