# ── Normalisation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ripper rather than a regex: `#` inside a string, a regex or a heredoc is not a
# comment, and a regex that thinks otherwise fails OPEN — it would report two
# copies as agreeing when they do not, which is the one failure mode this check
# must not have. :on_sp is whitespace BETWEEN tokens, so collapsing it ignores
# indentation and hash alignment while leaving string and heredoc content alone.
COMMENT_TOKENS = %i[on_comment on_embdoc on_embdoc_beg on_embdoc_end].freeze

# The `:code` normalisation, over a STRING rather than a file — so the fragment
# rule (T-120) compares a `def` with exactly the semantics `:code` compares a
# whole file with, rather than growing a second normaliser next to this one.
def code_norm(src)
  body = Ripper.lex(src).filter_map do |(_pos, type, tok)|
    next if COMMENT_TOKENS.include?(type)

    type == :on_sp ? " " : tok
  end.join
  body.lines.map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?).join("\n")
end

def code_digest(path) = Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(code_norm(File.read(path)))

def byte_digest(path) = Digest::SHA256.file(path).hexdigest

# ── A `:per_demo` subject whose copies have PARTIALLY CONVERGED (K-1262) ─────
#
# `:per_demo` means NOT COMPARED. Until 2026-08-31 the only thing anywhere in
# this file that noticed a `:per_demo` subject whose copies had stopped
# differing was the fragment rule's UNANIMITY tripwire one level down, and it
# fires when ALL held copies of a declared unit agree — only then. MEASURED by
# plant while T-146 was being built: converging TWO of
# `app/models/cart_mandate.rb`'s three `scope :referencing` copies onto a third
# left this script at EXIT 0; converging all three reddened it. At FILE level
# there was no tripwire at all, in either shape. So the state the manifest is
# LEAST able to describe — most of a group agreeing while one copy still
# differs — was invisible at both granularities, and that is precisely the state
# that means somebody hand-copied something.
#
# MEASURED at head over all 17 `:per_demo` paths, each path's copies grouped by
# the SHA256 of its {code_norm}: exactly TWO paths have a group of two or more,
# the same two the T-147 measurement found.
#
#   * `script/schema_flow.rb` — atablefor, philslist and tudu at 37 normalised
#     lines each, one digest. One driver, hand-copied three times, under an
#     entry whose own words were «asserts THAT demo's declared capabilities and
#     verb set»: true of the other four copies (46, 48, 59 and 23 normalised
#     lines) and untrue of these three.
#   * `app/operations/operation_result.rb` — two pairs, 9 and 3 normalised
#     lines, whose entry had already argued them out loud as coincidences.
#
# Those two are the whole reason the declaration has TWO KINDS rather than one:
#
#   rule: :lockstep    — these copies are ONE artefact that happens to live at N
#                        paths. They are HELD: a copy edited alone fails here,
#                        which is what turns 37 unwatched lines into watched
#                        ones. This is the answer for a hand-copied file.
#   rule: :coincidence — they agree today for unrelated reasons, and holding
#                        them would enshrine an accident. NOT held as a promise
#                        — but the group's MEMBERSHIP is: a demo JOINING it, or
#                        the group ceasing to agree, fails and the declaration
#                        is updated in the same commit. Both kinds are therefore
#                        checked exactly as hard; what differs is the DIAGNOSIS
#                        the next reader is handed, and the cap below.
#
# THE CAP IS THE ONE SUBSTANTIVE DIFFERENCE, and it is there because otherwise
# `:coincidence` would be a free way to switch this rule off — which is the
# undeclared-exclusion shape K-1262 belongs to. A `:coincidence` group may not
# exceed AGREE_COINCIDENCE_MAX_LINES normalised lines. MEASURED at head: the
# live coincidences are 9 and 3 normalised lines at file level and 7, 1 and 1 at
# unit level; the one lockstep group is 37. Twelve sits above every accident
# this tree actually contains and far below the one artefact, and above it «they
# happen to agree» stops being a credible reading of hand-copied code.
#
# THIS IS NOT K-792's OPTION B, which the `app/operations/operation_result.rb`
# entry forbids by name and which must stay forbidden. That was «compare every
# copy EXCEPT a named REGION of the file» — a partial COMPARISON, carving a
# region out of a file and holding the rest. This is a partial CONVERGENCE:
# whole copies, normalised whole, with the SUBSETS that agree named. No region
# is carved out of anything, and the entry's own argument — that the seven
# STATUSES maps are five different sets and even the one match is a coincidence
# to enshrine — is what the `:coincidence` kind exists to let it keep saying.
#
# ALL COPIES AGREEING IS NOT DECLARABLE, in either kind. That is not a partial
# convergence, it is a `:code` entry nobody has written yet, and it is reported
# as one — the rule the unit level has had since T-120, now at both levels.
AGREE_RULES = %i[lockstep coincidence].freeze
AGREE_COINCIDENCE_MAX_LINES = 12

# `bodies` is demo => normalised source; `declared` is the entry's `agree:` map,
# keyed by a demo list. Appends to `errors` and returns the number of declared
# groups it CONFIRMED against the tree — which the summary prints, so a rule that
# has stopped finding anything shows up as a number rather than as silence.
def agree_check(label, what, bodies, declared, errors)
  declared = (declared || {}).to_h { |demos, spec| [demos.sort, spec] }
  groups   = bodies.group_by { |_demo, norm| norm }
                   .values.select { |g| g.size >= 2 }
                   .to_h { |g| [g.map(&:first).sort, g.first[1]] }

  declared.each do |demos, spec|
    if demos.size < 2
      errors << "#{label}: `agree:` declares #{demos.inspect} — an agreement is two copies or more " \
                "(K-1262)."
      next
    end
    absent = demos - bodies.keys
    unless absent.empty?
      errors << "#{label}: `agree:` names #{absent.join(', ')}, which no longer contributes a " \
                "compared #{what} here. Update the declared set — a group that names a copy which is " \
                "gone is the record turning back into an assumption (K-1262)."
    end
    errors << "#{label}: `agree:` entry [#{demos.join(',')}] carries no `why:` (K-1262)." \
      if spec[:why].to_s.strip.empty?
    next if AGREE_RULES.include?(spec[:rule])

    errors << "#{label}: `agree:` entry [#{demos.join(',')}] has unknown `rule: " \
              "#{spec[:rule].inspect}` — the choice is `:lockstep` (one artefact, hand-copied, and " \
              "therefore HELD) or `:coincidence` (they agree today for unrelated reasons, recorded " \
              "and capped at #{AGREE_COINCIDENCE_MAX_LINES} normalised lines) (K-1262)."
  end

  widened = {}
  declared.each_key do |demos|
    next if groups.key?(demos)

    bigger = groups.keys.find { |g| (demos - g).empty? }
    widened[demos] = bigger if bigger
  end

  confirmed = 0
  groups.each do |demos, norm|
    if demos.size == bodies.size
      errors << "#{label}: declared `:per_demo` — genuinely different #{what}s sharing a name — but " \
                "ALL #{bodies.size} copies now agree once comments and whitespace are normalised " \
                "away (#{norm.lines.size} line(s)). That is not a partial convergence to declare in " \
                "`agree:`, it is a `:code` entry nobody has written: either declare it `:code` and " \
                "hold them there, or one copy lost what made it different (T-120, K-1262)."
      next
    end
    if (declared_key = widened.key(demos))
      joined = demos - declared_key
      errors << "#{label}: `agree:` declares [#{declared_key.join(',')}] and #{joined.join(', ')} " \
                "#{joined.size == 1 ? 'has' : 'have'} since joined — the agreeing group is now " \
                "[#{demos.join(',')}], #{norm.lines.size} normalised line(s). A demo joining a group " \
                "of copies that already agree is a NEW copy of something already hand-copied: widen " \
                "the declaration and re-argue it, or give that copy back what made it its own " \
                "(K-1262)."
      next
    end
    unless declared.key?(demos)
      # A declared group that has SHRUNK reports itself below, with the remedy
      # that fits (propagate the edit, or drop the group). Reporting its
      # surviving subset here as well would hand the next reader the WRONG
      # remedy — "declare [atablefor,tudu]" — for the same one-sided edit.
      next if declared.keys.any? { |d| (demos - d).empty? && !groups.key?(d) }

      errors << "#{label}: [#{demos.join(',')}] — #{demos.size} of #{bodies.size} copies — agree once " \
                "comments and whitespace are normalised away (#{norm.lines.size} normalised line(s)), " \
                "and nothing declares it. `:per_demo` means the copies are compared to NOTHING, so a " \
                "SUBSET that has quietly become one hand-copied #{what} is held by nothing while the " \
                "entry reads as #{bodies.size} per-demo implementations. Declare it in this entry's " \
                "`agree:` map — `:lockstep` if it is one artefact (then it is HELD), `:coincidence` " \
                "if they agree for unrelated reasons (then it is capped at " \
                "#{AGREE_COINCIDENCE_MAX_LINES} normalised lines) — with the reason (K-1262)."
      next
    end

    confirmed += 1
    spec = declared[demos]
    next unless spec[:rule] == :coincidence && norm.lines.size > AGREE_COINCIDENCE_MAX_LINES

    errors << "#{label}: `agree:` calls [#{demos.join(',')}] a `:coincidence`, but what they agree on " \
              "is #{norm.lines.size} normalised lines and the cap is #{AGREE_COINCIDENCE_MAX_LINES}. " \
              "At that size «they happen to agree» is not a credible reading of hand-copied code, and " \
              "an uncapped `:coincidence` is a free way to switch this rule off. Declare `:lockstep` " \
              "and hold them, or converge the rest and make the whole entry `:code` (K-1262)."
  end

  declared.each do |demos, spec|
    next if groups.key?(demos) || widened.key?(demos)
    next if (demos - bodies.keys).any?

    actual = bodies.select { |d, _| demos.include?(d) }
                   .group_by { |_d, norm| norm }
                   .values.map { |g| "[#{g.map(&:first).sort.join(',')}]" }.sort.join(" ")
    errors << if spec[:rule] == :lockstep
                "#{label}: `agree:` holds [#{demos.join(',')}] in lockstep — ONE #{what}, hand-copied " \
                  "— and they no longer agree: #{actual}. Propagate the edit to every copy in the " \
                  "group, or, if the divergence is real, drop the group from `agree:` in the same " \
                  "commit and say what made them diverge (K-1262)."
              else
                "#{label}: `agree:` records [#{demos.join(',')}] as a `:coincidence` and the " \
                  "coincidence has ended: #{actual}. Nothing is broken — drop the group from " \
                  "`agree:`, because a declaration that no longer describes the tree is the record " \
                  "turning back into an assumption (K-1262)."
              end
  end

  confirmed
end

# What the install generator would write today. `options` is the name the
# templates reference (the generator's Thor options hash); binding hands it to
# the ERB, so `<%= options[:schema].inspect %>` renders exactly as it does in
# `rails g kiosk:install` with no flags. Plain ERB, no trim mode — the
# templates use bare `<%= %>` substitution only.
def render_template(path)
  b = binding
  b.local_variable_set(:options, GENERATOR_DEFAULTS)
  ERB.new(File.read(path)).result(b)
end

# ── Magic comments (K-628) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The ONLY comments Ruby acts on. Restricted to the recognised set on purpose: a
# generic "leading `word: value` comment" rule would swallow ordinary prose
# ("# NOTE: ...") and start failing builds over wording, which is exactly what
# :code exists to avoid. Anything outside this list is inert to Ruby and stays
# invisible here.
#
# Only comments BEFORE the first real token count, because that is the only place
# Ruby honours them — a `# frozen_string_literal: true` further down is a plain
# comment and is correctly ignored.
MAGIC_COMMENTS = %w[encoding coding frozen_string_literal warn_indent shareable_constant_value].freeze
MAGIC_RE = /\A#\s*(?:-\*-\s*)?(#{Regexp.union(MAGIC_COMMENTS)})\s*:\s*([^\s;]+)/i

def magic_comments(path)
  found = []
  Ripper.lex(File.read(path)).each do |(_pos, type, tok)|
    case type
    when :on_sp, :on_nl, :on_ignored_nl then next
    when :on_comment
      m = tok.strip.match(MAGIC_RE)
      found << "#{m[1].downcase}: #{m[2].downcase}" if m
    else break # first real token: anything after this is inert to Ruby
    end
  end
  found.sort
end

# ── Discovery ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
demos = Dir[File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-*")].select { |d| File.directory?(d) }.sort
abort "check-demo-copies: no kiosk-demo-* directories under #{ROOT}" if demos.empty?

found = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = {} } # relative path => demo name => absolute path
demos.each do |dir|
  demo = File.basename(dir)
  SCAN_ROOTS.each do |sub|
    Dir[File.join(dir, sub, "**", "*.rb")].sort.each do |path|
      rel = path.delete_prefix("#{dir}/")
      next if rel.start_with?("lib/tasks/")

      found[rel][demo] = path
    end
  end
  EXTRA_SCANNED.each do |rel|
    path = File.join(dir, rel)
    found[rel][demo] = path if File.file?(path)
  end
end
dups = found.select { |_rel, copies| copies.size >= 2 }

# db/migrate again, grouped by timestamp-stripped basename this time (K-640):
# migration_template stamps each demo's copy with its own UTC timestamp, so the
# same logical migration sits at different relative paths in different demos
# (getgrocery's create_kiosk_mandates is at …131463 where hoteling's is
# …131462) — grouping by basename is what lets the template rule see them as
# one file where the relative-path rule above cannot.
migrations = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = {} } # basename sans timestamp => demo name => absolute path
demos.each do |dir|
  Dir[File.join(dir, "db/migrate", "*.rb")].sort.each do |path|
    migrations[File.basename(path).sub(/\A\d+_/, "")][File.basename(dir)] = path
  end
end
migrations.default_proc = nil

if ARGV.include?("--list")
  puts format("%-64s %-9s %s", "PATH", "RULE", "DEMOS")
  COPIES.sort.each do |rel, entry|
    copies = dups[rel] || {}
    marks  = copies.keys.map { |d| d.delete_prefix("kiosk-demo-") + (entry[:except]&.key?(d.delete_prefix("kiosk-demo-")) ? "*" : "") }
    puts format("%-64s %-9s %s", rel, entry[:rule], marks.join(" "))
  end
  puts "\n* = declared exception (see the `except:` reason in bin/check-demo-copies)"
  puts
  puts format("%-44s %-42s %s", "TEMPLATE (kiosk:install)", "EMITS", "STATE")
  GENERATOR_TEMPLATES.sort.each do |name, entry|
    state = entry[:not_compared] ? "not compared" : "byte-match, all seven"
    shipped = (migrations[entry[:emits]] || {}).keys
    puts format("%-44s %-42s %s%s", name, entry[:emits] || "—", state,
                shipped.empty? ? "" : " (#{shipped.map { |d| d.delete_prefix('kiosk-demo-') }.sort.join(' ')})")
  end
  puts
  puts format("%-44s %-9s %s", "MIGRATION PAIR (K-648, sans timestamp)", "RULE", "STATE")
  MIGRATION_PAIRS.sort.each do |base, entry|
    state  = entry[:drift] ? "drift pending triage" : "held"
    spread = (migrations[base] || {}).map { |d, p| "#{d.delete_prefix('kiosk-demo-')}@#{File.basename(p)[/\A\d+/]}" }.sort
    puts format("%-44s %-9s %s (%s)", base, entry[:rule], state, spread.join(" "))
  end
  exit 0
end

errors = []
short = ->(demo) { demo.delete_prefix("kiosk-demo-") }

# Declared `agree:` groups CONFIRMED against the tree this run, over both
# granularities. See the K-1262 banner above {agree_check} for the vacuity arm
# this number exists to feed.
agree_groups = 0

# ── Undeclared and stale manifest entries ────────────────────────────────────
(dups.keys - COPIES.keys).sort.each do |rel|
  copies = dups[rel]
  groups = copies.group_by { |_demo, path| code_digest(path) }
                 .values.map { |g| "[#{g.map { |d, _| short.(d) }.sort.join(',')}]" }
  errors << "#{rel}: exists in #{copies.size} demos (#{copies.keys.map(&short).sort.join(', ')}) but is not " \
            "declared in bin/check-demo-copies. Add an entry with a rule and a reason " \
            "(:identical / :code / :per_demo). Ignoring comments and whitespace the copies group as " \
            "#{groups.join(' ')}."
end

# ── The recorded exclusion must stay true (K-629) ────────────────────────────
#
# Documentary, but not unchecked: an entry naming a path no longer duplicated,
# or one carrying no reason, is the record turning back into an assumption.
SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED.each do |rel, why|
  errors << "#{rel}: SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED entry carries no reason" if why.to_s.strip.empty?

  ships = demos.count { |dir| File.exist?(File.join(dir, rel)) }
  next if ships >= 2

  errors << "#{rel}: recorded in SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED as a skeleton file this check does not " \
            "compare, but only #{ships} demo ships it — it is not a duplicate any more. Drop the entry."
end

overlap = SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED.keys & COPIES.keys
errors << "#{overlap.join(', ')}: declared BOTH in COPIES and in SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED — a path is " \
          "either compared or recorded as not compared, not both." if overlap.any?

(COPIES.keys - dups.keys).sort.each do |rel|
  where = found[rel]&.keys&.map(&short) || []
  errors << "#{rel}: declared in bin/check-demo-copies but " +
            (where.empty? ? "no demo ships it any more — drop the entry." \
                          : "only #{where.join(', ')} ships it — drop the entry.")
end

# ── The one comparison this whole file exists for ────────────────────────────
COPIES.each do |rel, entry|
  copies = dups[rel] or next

  errors << "#{rel}: manifest entry carries no `why:` reason" if entry[:why].to_s.strip.empty?
  unless %i[identical code per_demo].include?(entry[:rule])
    errors << "#{rel}: unknown rule #{entry[:rule].inspect}"
    next
  end

  present = copies.keys.map(&short)

  # ── The declared SET (K-860, K-883) ──────────────────────────────────────
  # Only for entries that declare one. An unlisted demo shipping the file is a
  # copy nobody reasoned about — which is how the two consumerless copies got
  # here — and a listed demo that no longer ships it is the record turning back
  # into an assumption, the same failure SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED is checked for.
  if entry[:ships]
    stray = present - entry[:ships]
    gone  = entry[:ships] - present
    unless stray.empty?
      errors << "#{rel}: #{stray.sort.join(', ')} #{stray.size == 1 ? 'ships' : 'ship'} this file " \
                "but #{stray.size == 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} not in its declared `ships:` set " \
                "(#{entry[:ships].sort.join(', ')}). A copy is not free: either give that demo a " \
                "driver that USES it and add it to `ships:` with the reason, or delete the copy."
    end
    unless gone.empty?
      errors << "#{rel}: `ships:` names #{gone.sort.join(', ')}, which no longer ship#{gone.size == 1 ? 's' : ''} " \
                "this file — update the declared set."
    end
  end

  excepted = entry[:except] || {}
  excepted.each do |demo, reason|
    errors << "#{rel}: `except:` reason for #{demo} is blank" if reason.to_s.strip.empty?
    errors << "#{rel}: stale `except:` entry for #{demo} — that demo does not ship this file" \
      unless present.include?(demo)
  end
  if entry[:agree] && entry[:rule] != :per_demo
    errors << "#{rel}: `agree:` names the copies of an UNCOMPARED entry that have converged; this " \
              "entry is `:#{entry[:rule]}`, so every copy is already compared against every other " \
              "one and the map records nothing. Drop it (K-1262)."
  end

  if entry[:rule] == :per_demo
    errors << "#{rel}: :per_demo entries are not compared, so `except:` means nothing here" if excepted.any?
    agree_groups += agree_check(rel, "file", copies.to_h { |demo, path| [short.(demo), code_norm(File.read(path))] },
                                entry[:agree], errors)
    next
  end

  digest = entry[:rule] == :identical ? method(:byte_digest) : method(:code_digest)
  compared, skipped = copies.partition { |demo, _| !excepted.key?(short.(demo)) }.map(&:to_h)

  if compared.size < 2
    errors << "#{rel}: every copy but one is excepted — the entry guards nothing. Either drop an " \
              "exception or make the rule :per_demo."
    next
  end

  groups = compared.group_by { |_demo, path| digest.call(path) }
  if groups.size > 1
    how = entry[:rule] == :identical ? "byte-for-byte" : "once comments and whitespace are ignored"
    listed = groups.values.map { |g| "[#{g.map { |d, _| short.(d) }.sort.join(',')}]" }.join(" ")
    errors << "#{rel}: declared :#{entry[:rule]} — the copies must agree #{how}, and they do not: " \
              "#{listed}. Propagate the edit to every copy, or, if the divergence is deliberate, " \
              "record it in this file's `except:` map with the reason."
  end

  # ── The comment `:code` does NOT ignore (K-628) ────────────────────────────
  #
  # code_digest cannot see this: Ripper drops magic comments with every other
  # comment, so a copy that lost `# frozen_string_literal: true` groups with one
  # that kept it. :identical is not checked here — byte equality already implies
  # it, and a second error saying the same thing helps nobody.
  magic = ->(path) { magic_comments(path) }
  if entry[:rule] == :code
    magic_groups = compared.group_by { |_demo, path| magic.call(path) }
    if magic_groups.size > 1
      listed = magic_groups.map do |comments, g|
        "[#{g.map { |d, _| short.(d) }.sort.join(',')}] #{comments.empty? ? '(none)' : comments.join(', ')}"
      end.join(" vs ")
      errors << "#{rel}: declared :code — prose may differ per demo, MAGIC comments may not, because " \
                "they change how the file runs: #{listed}. Add the missing one to every copy (or " \
                "remove it from all of them); `except:` is for real per-demo behaviour, not for this."
    end
  end

  # An exception that is no longer needed is a lie the next reader will believe.
  next unless groups.size == 1

  canonical = groups.keys.first
  canonical_magic = entry[:rule] == :code ? magic.call(compared.values.first) : nil
  skipped.each do |demo, path|
    next unless digest.call(path) == canonical
    next if canonical_magic && magic.call(path) != canonical_magic

    errors << "#{rel}: `except:` for #{short.(demo)} is stale — its copy now agrees with the others. " \
              "Drop the exception so the file is guarded."
  end
end

# ── Template ↔ copy: the drift the lockstep rule cannot see (K-640) ──────────
#
# For each declared template, render it with the generator defaults and require
# every demo copy to byte-match the render — so an engine-side schema edit that
# is not propagated to the demos (or a hand-edit to a copy that is not lifted
# back into the template) fails here instead of surfacing as a runtime column
# error in whichever demo nobody ran.
tpl_files = Dir[File.join(ROOT, GENERATOR_TEMPLATES_DIR, "*.tt")].map { |p| File.basename(p) }

(tpl_files - GENERATOR_TEMPLATES.keys).sort.each do |name|
  errors << "#{GENERATOR_TEMPLATES_DIR}/#{name}: new install-generator template not declared in " \
            "bin/check-demo-copies GENERATOR_TEMPLATES. Add an entry saying what it emits (or why " \
            "it is not compared), so the demos cannot skip regenerating it silently."
end
(GENERATOR_TEMPLATES.keys - tpl_files).sort.each do |name|
  errors << "#{name}: declared in GENERATOR_TEMPLATES but #{GENERATOR_TEMPLATES_DIR} has no such " \
            "template any more — drop the entry."
end

# ── NO LEDGER ID IN A SHIPPED GENERATOR TEMPLATE (K-1298) ────────────────────
#
# These templates are not read, they are WRITTEN -- `kiosk:install` copies them
# into a third party's own repository. A `(K-830)` in one of them is therefore
# not the demo-comment leak one directory over: it lands in SOMEBODY ELSE'S
# codebase, pointing at a private ledger they cannot open and never will.
#
# The demo tree was scrubbed of ledger ids on 2026-09-05 and this survived it,
# because the scrub's file set was `kiosk-demo-*` and this file is not there.
# Measured when the arm was written: one occurrence across the six templates,
# and zero after. A live TODO/FIXME still requires its id -- that is the
# constitution's rule -- so the ban is on the id, not on the marker that needs
# one, and a template growing a real TODO should declare it here rather than
# losing the reference that makes it actionable.
# One spelling of «a ledger id», shared by this rule and the demo-tree rule
# below it, because two copies of one pattern is two things to be wrong in
# different ways -- and the second one to rot would rot silently, since both
# rules are BANS whose obedience and whose blindness print the same zero.
LEDGER_ID_RE = /\b[KT]-\d{3,4}\b/

tpl_files.sort.each do |name|
  path = File.join(GENERATOR_TEMPLATES_DIR, name)
  File.read(File.join(ROOT, path)).each_line.with_index(1) do |line, lineno|
    next unless line =~ LEDGER_ID_RE
    next if line =~ /\b(TODO|FIXME)\b/

    errors << "#{path}:#{lineno}: a ledger id in a template the install generator WRITES INTO A " \
              "CONSUMER'S REPOSITORY -- they cannot resolve it and it is not theirs to carry. " \
              "Say the reason instead of citing the row: #{line.strip[0, 90]}"
  end
end

# ── NO LEDGER ID IN CODE A DEMO RUNS (K-1383) ────────────────────────────────
#
# THE RULE ABOVE IS ONE DIRECTORY TOO NARROW. It bans a ledger id in the six
# install-generator templates, because those are WRITTEN into a third party's
# repository. The demo tree next door has the same reader problem for a
# different reason: a `K-1099` inside a `desc`, an `abort`, a task banner or an
# assertion label is printed AT A TERMINAL, to an operator who cannot open the
# ledger it names and never will.
#
# WHY IT IS THIS NARROW HERE, AND WHAT HOLDS THE REST. This rule speaks for one
# reader: the operator at a terminal. A ledger id in a `desc`, a banner, an
# `abort` or an assertion label reaches somebody's screen, and that is a
# different failure from a citation in prose, so it keeps its own message and
# its own remedy.
#
# COMMENTS ARE NO LONGER LEFT ALONE -- they are held next door, by
# `bin/check-shipped-archaeology`, which bans a ledger id, an ADR reference and
# Cyrillic prose in the demo tree's comments and prose alike, on the argument
# that a demo is the first thing an operator opens and its comments are read
# exactly as a shipped gem's are. This file therefore no longer counts demo
# comment citations as evidence that its pattern still works: at head there are
# NONE, by design. Phil's standing exemption -- `bin/`, `CHANGELOG.md`,
# `CLAUDE.md` -- is unchanged in both guards.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, MEASURED. K-1323's sweep finished; nothing held it
# afterwards. Its own two measurements are 568 occurrences immediately after an
# earlier wave and 759 when the fix started -- 51 occurrences of 12 distinct ids
# re-introduced in about a day, ALL of it by ordinary fix waves rather than by
# anyone deciding to. Re-measured when this rule was written: **24 occurrences
# under `kiosk-demo-*` (git grep -P, PCRE -- an ERE `\b` here matches NOTHING and
# exits 1), 19 of them in Ruby this rule scans and every one of those in a
# COMMENT, zero in code.** So the ban starts at zero survivors and needs no
# exemption list at all; the day it needs one, the entry goes here with its
# reason, exactly like every other manifest in this file.
#
# COMMENT versus CODE IS ASKED OF RIPPER, NOT OF A REGEX. `^\s*#` misses a
# trailing comment and a `#` inside a string literal or a heredoc is not a
# comment at all; both mistakes are silent and both point the wrong way. The
# lexer answers exactly the question the rule asks, and a file it cannot lex is
# an ERROR rather than a skip -- the fail-open discipline this file uses
# everywhere else.
#
# WHAT IS NOT SCANNED, declared rather than assumed:
#   * `kiosk-demo-*/bin/` -- Phil's standing exemption, alongside CHANGELOG.md
#     and CLAUDE.md. Those are generator binstubs and maintainer documents, not
#     something a demo prints.
#   * `.md`, `.html`, `.erb`, `.sql` -- not Ruby, so «comment» means a different
#     thing in each and Ripper cannot answer for them. The three `<!-- derived:
#     -->` declarations and the two `before-after.md` transcript lines that carry
#     an id today are all in that set, and all three are MACHINE-READ
#     declarations `bin/check-demo-derivations` holds.
#   * `spec/` and `test/` -- developer surfaces. An operator never runs them, and
#     the constitution's own TODO/FIXME rule REQUIRES an id there.
#
# THE VACUITY GUARDS, and note the polarity: this is a BAN, so «no violations»
# is the tree being clean rather than the rule being switched off. Two things
# must therefore be asserted about the MECHANISM instead. The file count catches
# a glob that stopped matching or a lexer that stopped running. The FIXTURE
# catches the pattern itself rotting.
#
# THE FIXTURE IS OWNED BY THIS RULE, and it is the second spelling of that arm.
# The first counted demo comment citations and asserted the count was positive,
# which was evidence only while the demo tree still carried some. It stopped
# being evidence the day `bin/check-shipped-archaeology` took the comment half
# and the tree was scrubbed to zero -- the arm went red for an honest reason,
# exactly as it said it would, and this is the replacement it demanded rather
# than the deletion it forbade. The arm before that was worse still: it cited a
# LIVE sentence, the seven `config/routes/kiosk.rb` copies of «(T-074 = A)»,
# and T-186 deleted that sentence as retired-wire narration.
#
# {DEMO_ID_FIXTURES} is therefore neither a census nor a citation. It is the
# shapes people write, held in this file, asserted to match {LEDGER_ID_RE} and
# asserted to be classified by Ripper the way this rule needs -- an id in a
# comment token is a comment, an id in a string literal is not. A pattern that
# stops matching them, or a lexer that stops separating them, fails here and
# says which.
DEMO_ID_ROOTS = %w[app lib script config db].freeze
DEMO_ID_TOP   = %w[Rakefile config.ru].freeze

# The shapes people write, owned by this rule. Each must match LEDGER_ID_RE,
# and the pair below must stay separable by the lexer.
DEMO_ID_FIXTURES = [
  "# The past-slot filter, K-480.",
  "# widened by T-158",
  "  desc \"reset the demo\" # K-1099",
  "abort(\"see K-1099\")"
].freeze
DEMO_ID_IN_COMMENT = "# The past-slot filter, K-480.\n"
DEMO_ID_IN_STRING  = "MSG = \"blocked by K-480\"\n"

demo_id_files   = 0
demo_id_comment = 0
demo_id_unlexed = []

demos.each do |dir|
  paths = DEMO_ID_ROOTS.flat_map { |r| Dir[File.join(dir, r, "**", "*.{rb,rake,ru}")] } +
          DEMO_ID_TOP.map { |n| File.join(dir, n) }.select { |p| File.file?(p) }
  paths.sort.each do |abs|
    rel = abs.delete_prefix("#{ROOT}/")
    src = File.read(abs)
    demo_id_files += 1
    next unless src.match?(LEDGER_ID_RE)

    tokens =
      begin
        Ripper.lex(src)
      rescue StandardError
        nil
      end
    if tokens.nil? || tokens.empty?
      demo_id_unlexed << rel
      next
    end

    tokens.each do |(pos, type, tok, _state)|
      next unless tok.to_s.match?(LEDGER_ID_RE)

      if type == :on_comment
        demo_id_comment += 1
        next
      end

      errors << "#{rel}:#{pos[0]}: a ledger id OUTSIDE a comment, in code this demo RUNS -- it " \
                "reaches an operator's terminal (a `desc` that `rake -D` prints, a task banner, an " \
                "`abort`, an assertion label) and names a private ledger row they cannot open " \
                "(K-1383, the class K-1323 swept and nothing held afterwards). Say what the control " \
                "IS rather than which row filed it — «past-slot filter», not «K-480» — and keep the " \
                "id in a comment beside it if the provenance is worth carrying: " \
                "#{tok.to_s.strip[0, 90]}"
    end
  end
end

if demo_id_files.zero?
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1383 ledger-id rule read 0 Ruby file(s) under " \
            "kiosk-demo-*/{#{DEMO_ID_ROOTS.join(',')}} — the glob has stopped matching, and a BAN " \
            "over an empty corpus reports the same clean zero as an obeyed one, forever."
end
unless demo_id_unlexed.empty?
  errors << "#{demo_id_unlexed.sort.join(', ')}: Ripper returned no tokens for " \
            "#{demo_id_unlexed.one? ? 'this file' : 'these files'}, which carry a ledger id somewhere — " \
            "so the K-1383 rule could not tell a comment from a string in them and did not check them " \
            "at all. A file this rule cannot read must not pass silently."
end
# VACUITY, against a fixture this rule owns rather than against the tree. See
# the paragraph above for why the tree stopped being evidence.
dead_fixtures = DEMO_ID_FIXTURES.reject { _1.match?(LEDGER_ID_RE) }
unless dead_fixtures.empty?
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1383 ledger-id pattern no longer matches its own fixture " \
            "(#{dead_fixtures.map(&:inspect).join(', ')}). LEDGER_ID_RE has stopped matching a " \
            "shape people write, so this ban is holding nothing and its clean zero means nothing."
end

fixture_tokens = ->(src) {
  (Ripper.lex(src) || []).select { |(_pos, _type, tok, _st)| tok.to_s.match?(LEDGER_ID_RE) }
}
comment_toks = fixture_tokens.call(DEMO_ID_IN_COMMENT)
string_toks  = fixture_tokens.call(DEMO_ID_IN_STRING)
unless comment_toks.size == 1 && comment_toks.first[1] == :on_comment &&
       string_toks.size == 1 && string_toks.first[1] != :on_comment
  errors << "check-demo-copies: Ripper no longer separates an id in a COMMENT from an id in a " \
            "STRING on this rule's own fixtures (comment → #{comment_toks.map { _1[1] }.inspect}, " \
            "string → #{string_toks.map { _1[1] }.inspect}). The whole ban is that distinction, " \
            "so it cannot be assumed."
end

operator_demos = demos.map { |d| File.basename(d) } - NON_OPERATOR_DEMOS

GENERATOR_TEMPLATES.each do |name, entry|
  next unless tpl_files.include?(name)

  if entry[:not_compared]
    errors << "#{name}: `not_compared:` carries no reason" if entry[:not_compared].to_s.strip.empty?
    next
  end
  if entry[:emits].to_s.strip.empty?
    errors << "#{name}: entry declares neither `emits:` nor `not_compared:` — say what the " \
              "template emits, or why its output is not compared."
    next
  end

  emits  = entry[:emits]
  copies = migrations[emits] || {}
  if copies.empty?
    errors << "#{name}: no demo ships a db/migrate copy of #{emits} — if the demos dropped the " \
              "migration, drop (or `not_compared:`) the entry with the reason. A RENAME is not a " \
              "state this check has any more (K-646): the demos ship the generator's own " \
              "basenames, so a copy under another name is a copy that was not regenerated."
    next
  end

  # ONE_SET_EVERYWHERE (K-646). Every operator demo ships every canonical
  # migration, because every operator demo IS one `kiosk:install` run. A demo
  # missing one is the shape the fleet drifted into before — subsets nobody
  # could adjudicate — and a NEW demo scaffolded from a stale engine is the
  # same failure arriving from the other direction.
  missing = operator_demos - copies.keys
  unless missing.empty?
    errors << "#{name}: #{missing.map(&short).sort.join(', ')} #{missing.size == 1 ? 'ships' : 'ship'} " \
              "no copy of #{emits}, but every operator demo must ship every canonical migration " \
              "(K-646 — the demos are one `kiosk:install` run, not per-demo subsets). Re-run the " \
              "generator into #{missing.size == 1 ? 'that demo' : 'those demos'} and commit the " \
              "output, or — if the migration genuinely left the canonical set — drop the template " \
              "and this entry together."
  end
  extra = copies.keys & NON_OPERATOR_DEMOS
  unless extra.empty?
    errors << "#{name}: #{extra.map(&short).sort.join(', ')} ships #{emits}, but it is declared a " \
              "NON-operator demo (it adopts no Kiosk engine schema). Either it is an operator now " \
              "— drop it from NON_OPERATOR_DEMOS — or the migration does not belong to it."
  end

  rendered = render_template(File.join(ROOT, GENERATOR_TEMPLATES_DIR, name))
  mismatched = copies.reject { |_demo, path| File.read(path) == rendered }
  next if mismatched.empty?

  demo, path = mismatched.first
  a = rendered.lines
  b = File.read(path).lines
  i = (0...[a.size, b.size].max).find { |j| a[j] != b[j] }
  errors << "#{GENERATOR_TEMPLATES_DIR}/#{name}: rendered with the generator's default options " \
            "it must byte-match every demo copy of #{emits}, and " \
            "#{mismatched.keys.map(&short).sort.join(', ')} " \
            "#{mismatched.size == 1 ? 'disagrees' : 'disagree'} (first difference at line " \
            "#{i + 1}: template renders #{(a[i] || '<nothing>').chomp.strip.inspect}, " \
            "#{short.(demo)} has #{(b[i] || '<nothing>').chomp.strip.inspect}). The engine's " \
            "generator output and the demos moved apart — regenerate the demos' copies, or lift " \
            "the copies' edit back into the template, so they move as ONE change. There is NO " \
            "way to record this as a known exception any more (K-646): the four that could be " \
            "recorded in 2026-08 were all still open three months later, which is what a " \
            "recordable exception actually buys."
end

# ── Cross-demo singleton pairs: the migrations neither rule above sees (K-648)
#
# Matched by timestamp-stripped basename, like the template rule — but these
# have no template, so the copies are held against EACH OTHER, like the
# lockstep rule. Detection first: an undeclared pair fails, so a hand-written
# migration copied into a second demo at its own timestamp cannot appear
# silently — the same property the path rule gives paths.
template_basenames = GENERATOR_TEMPLATES.filter_map { |_name, e| e[:emits] }

migrations.each do |base, copies|
  next if copies.size < 2
  next if template_basenames.include?(base)
  # Copies all at ONE shared relative path are the lockstep rule's territory —
  # declared in COPIES, or already failed above as an undeclared duplicate.
  next if copies.values.map { |p| File.basename(p) }.uniq.size == 1
  next if MIGRATION_PAIRS.key?(base)

  spread = copies.map { |d, p| "#{short.(d)}@#{File.basename(p)[/\A\d+/]}" }.sort
  errors << "db/migrate/*_#{base}: ships in #{copies.size} demos at demo-specific timestamps " \
            "(#{spread.join(', ')}) — invisible to the relative-path rule and emitted by no " \
            "install-generator template, so the copies are compared to NOTHING. Declare the " \
            "basename in MIGRATION_PAIRS with a rule and a reason."
end

MIGRATION_PAIRS.each do |base, entry|
  copies = migrations[base] || {}
  label = "db/migrate/*_#{base}"

  errors << "#{label}: MIGRATION_PAIRS entry carries no `why:` reason" if entry[:why].to_s.strip.empty?
  unless %i[identical code].include?(entry[:rule])
    errors << "#{label}: unknown rule #{entry[:rule].inspect} — MIGRATION_PAIRS takes :identical " \
              "or :code (a :per_demo migration pair would be two unrelated files sharing a name; " \
              "record that in the entry's why: by splitting the basenames instead)."
    next
  end
  if copies.size < 2
    errors << "#{label}: declared in MIGRATION_PAIRS but " +
              (copies.empty? ? "no demo ships it any more — drop the entry." \
                             : "only #{copies.keys.map(&short).join(', ')} ships it — drop the entry.")
    next
  end
  if template_basenames.include?(base)
    errors << "#{label}: declared in MIGRATION_PAIRS but GENERATOR_TEMPLATES already holds every " \
              "copy against its template — drop the entry, one declaration per file."
    next
  end
  if copies.values.map { |p| File.basename(p) }.uniq.size == 1
    errors << "#{label}: declared in MIGRATION_PAIRS but every copy now sits at the same relative " \
              "path, which the lockstep rule compares — declare the path in COPIES and drop this " \
              "entry."
    next
  end

  digest = entry[:rule] == :identical ? method(:byte_digest) : method(:code_digest)
  groups = copies.group_by { |_demo, path| digest.call(path) }
  how    = entry[:rule] == :identical ? "byte-for-byte" : "once comments and whitespace are ignored"
  if entry[:drift]
    errors << "#{label}: `drift:` carries no reason" if entry[:drift].to_s.strip.empty?
    if groups.size == 1
      errors << "#{label}: `drift:` is stale — the copies now agree #{how}. Drop the drift so the " \
                "pair is guarded."
    end
  elsif groups.size > 1
    listed = groups.values.map { |g| "[#{g.map { |d, _| short.(d) }.sort.join(',')}]" }.join(" ")
    errors << "#{label}: declared :#{entry[:rule]} — the copies must agree #{how}, and they do " \
              "not: #{listed}. Propagate the edit to every copy, or, if the divergence is " \
              "deliberate, record it as a `drift:` reason with what differs and why."
  end

  # Same K-628 rule as the lockstep loop: prose may differ, MAGIC comments not.
  if entry[:rule] == :code
    magic_groups = copies.group_by { |_demo, path| magic_comments(path) }
    if magic_groups.size > 1
      listed = magic_groups.map do |comments, g|
        "[#{g.map { |d, _| short.(d) }.sort.join(',')}] #{comments.empty? ? '(none)' : comments.join(', ')}"
      end.join(" vs ")
      errors << "#{label}: declared :code — prose may differ per demo, MAGIC comments may not, " \
                "because they change how the file runs: #{listed}."
    end
  end
end

# ── The app/services autoload-once declaration (K-502, re-anchored by T-189) ─
#
# A demo that names an `app/services` constant from
# `config/initializers/kiosk.rb` — an adapter, a broker, a stub PSP — can only
# do so because app/services is in `config.autoload_once_paths`. Rails sets the
# RELOADABLE autoloader up in its `finisher`, AFTER config/initializers have
# run, so a constant in a normal autoload path is simply not resolvable from an
# initializer; the once loader is set up in `bootstrap`, first. Drop that one
# line and the demo aborts at boot with `NameError: uninitialized constant`.
#
# `config/application.rb` is :per_demo — the module name IS the demo name — so
# nothing above compares this line, and it is the deletable-and-silently-fatal
# kind: no static reader of the initializer would notice. Honest scope: this
# asserts the declaration is PRESENT, not that a boot succeeds; each demo's own
# CI job boots the app.
#
# THE SUBJECT IS DERIVED, NOT NAMED. This rule used to hang off
# `app/services/pow_difficulty.rb`, which shipped in all seven demos and made
# the answer "all seven" a constant. T-189 promoted that module into
# kiosk-pow-equihash, so three demos now have an EMPTY app/services and the
# once-path there would be a config line loading nothing. So the demo set is
# read from the tree — which constants app/services actually defines, and
# whether the initializer names one — and the rule is TWO-SIDED: a demo that
# needs the line must have it, and a demo that does not must not carry it. The
# second half is what a hand-named subject could never have caught.
AUTOLOAD_ONCE_PATTERN = /autoload_once_paths\s*<<\s*Rails\.root\.join\("app\/services"\)/

autoload_once_checked = 0
demos.each do |dir|
  demo     = File.basename(dir)
  app_path = File.join(dir, "config/application.rb")
  init     = File.join(dir, "config/initializers/kiosk.rb")
  next unless File.exist?(app_path) && File.exist?(init)

  # Zeitwerk's own inflection: one constant per file, camelised from the name.
  service_constants = Dir[File.join(dir, "app/services/*.rb")]
                      .map { |f| File.basename(f, ".rb").split("_").map(&:capitalize).join }
  initializer = File.read(init)
  named = service_constants.select { |c| initializer.match?(/(?<![A-Za-z0-9_:])#{Regexp.escape(c)}\b/) }
  declared = File.read(app_path).match?(AUTOLOAD_ONCE_PATTERN)
  autoload_once_checked += 1

  if named.any? && !declared
    errors << "#{short.(demo)}: config/initializers/kiosk.rb names #{named.sort.join(', ')} from " \
              "app/services but config/application.rb does not put app/services in " \
              "config.autoload_once_paths — the reloadable autoloader is set up AFTER " \
              "config/initializers run, so that reference would abort the boot with NameError (K-502)"
  elsif named.empty? && declared
    errors << "#{short.(demo)}: config/application.rb puts app/services in " \
              "config.autoload_once_paths, but config/initializers/kiosk.rb names no constant " \
              "defined there#{service_constants.empty? ? ' and the directory is empty' : ''} — the " \
              "once-path is a config line loading nothing, and a non-reloadable path is not free " \
              "(T-189)"
  end
end

if autoload_once_checked.zero?
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-502 autoload-once rule read NO demo — it resolves " \
            "config/application.rb and config/initializers/kiosk.rb per demo, and a rule with " \
            "nothing to read passes everything."
end

# ── What is LEFT in a per-demo operation_result.rb (K-792) ───────────────────
#
# `app/operations/operation_result.rb` is declared :per_demo, and :per_demo
# means NOT COMPARED. That was a real gap while the file carried ~40 lines of
# shared constructor: seven copies of code nothing held together. K-792 closed
# it by PROMOTION, not by a compare rule — the constructor and the
# ok/refused/status trio now ship once, as Kiosk::OperationResult in
# kiosk-server, and each demo subclasses it.
#
# So this block guards the promotion rather than the file's contents. It asserts
# the three properties that make "not compared" honest again:
#
#   1. the copy actually SUBCLASSES the gem's class — a demo that quietly went
#      back to a standalone `class OperationResult` would be re-growing the
#      duplication this closed, and every gate would stay green;
#   2. it declares its OWN STATUSES — the base ships an empty map on purpose, so
#      a demo that inherits it silently refuses nothing and only fails at the
#      moment a refusal is rendered;
#   3. it defines NO methods at all. That is the property that matters: a file
#      that may hold only a subclass line and a constant has nowhere for
#      uncompared behaviour to hide, which is why :per_demo is the right rule
#      for what is left rather than a hole this check is looking away from.
OPERATION_RESULT = "app/operations/operation_result.rb"

(dups[OPERATION_RESULT] || {}).each do |demo, path|
  src = File.read(path)

  unless src.match?(/^class\s+OperationResult\s*<\s*Kiosk::OperationResult\b/)
    errors << "#{short.(demo)}: #{OPERATION_RESULT} does not subclass Kiosk::OperationResult — the " \
              "shared half (the constructor and the ok/refused/status trio) lives in kiosk-server " \
              "since K-792, and a standalone class here re-grows the seven-copy duplication that " \
              "promotion removed. This file is :per_demo, i.e. NOT compared, so nothing else would " \
              "notice."
  end

  unless src.match?(/^\s+STATUSES\s*=/)
    errors << "#{short.(demo)}: #{OPERATION_RESULT} declares no STATUSES map — Kiosk::OperationResult " \
              "ships an EMPTY one on purpose (it does not know this app's refusals), so inheriting it " \
              "means every refusal raises KeyError at render time instead of failing here."
  end

  defined_methods = Ripper.lex(src).each_cons(3).filter_map do |(_, t1, tok1), (_, t2, _), (_, _, tok3)|
    next unless t1 == :on_kw && tok1 == "def"

    t2 == :on_sp ? tok3 : nil
  end
  next if defined_methods.empty?

  errors << "#{short.(demo)}: #{OPERATION_RESULT} defines #{defined_methods.map(&:inspect).join(', ')} " \
            "— this file is :per_demo (NOT compared), so it may hold ONLY the subclass line and its " \
            "own STATUSES map. Behaviour every demo shares goes into Kiosk::OperationResult in " \
            "kiosk-server (K-792); behaviour one verb needs goes into that verb's Operation."
end

# ── application_controller.rb's PROSE must stay true (K-1021) ────────────────
#
# `app/controllers/application_controller.rb` is declared `:code`, and `:code`
# compares files MODULO COMMENTS — which is the right rule for a file whose
# header must name its own demo's controllers, and is exactly how one FALSE
# sentence survived in seven demos plus the e2e fixture at once. Until K-1017
# read it, every copy said «The Kiosk wire controllers ship their own bases
# inside kiosk-server». They do not: kiosk-server ships a MIXIN, and each demo's
# `Kiosk::*Controller` names its own superclass. Eight copies, no gate — because
# the script's whole rule vocabulary (`:identical`, `:code`, `:per_demo`) is
# about CODE, so no comment in any copied file was guarded anywhere.
#
# The general lesson, recorded once because it outlives this row: `:code` means
# THE PROSE IS UNGUARDED, so a per-demo comment asserting a fact about the tree
# needs its own assertion or it rots silently in N copies at once.
#
# So this block makes the one load-bearing sentence mechanical. Each copy must
# carry, in its comments, a sentence of the form
#
#     here <Kiosk::AController and Kiosk::BController> subclass <SUPERCLASS>
#
# where <SUPERCLASS> is either the literal «THIS class» (meaning
# ApplicationController, the class the file itself defines) or a constant path
# such as ActionController::API. Both halves are then checked against the tree,
# NOT against a list kept here: the controllers are globbed off disk and their
# superclasses read out of their own `class … < …` lines. A copy that names the
# wrong controllers, names the wrong superclass, or does not carry the sentence
# at all fails — and the pre-K-1017 sentence fails on the last of those three,
# because it named no controller and no superclass.
#
# The e2e harness's fixture carried the same sentence and is checked the same
# way, from `e2e/fixtures/*_controller.rb` — it is not a demo, so no rule above
# can see it, and it is the eighth copy the false sentence reached.
PROSE_SUPERCLASS_SENTENCE = /
  \bhere\s+
  (?<names>Kiosk::[A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:\s*(?:,|and)\s*Kiosk::[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)
  \s+subclass(?:es)?\s+
  (?<super>THIS\ class|[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:::[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)
/x

# Every `class Kiosk::Foo < Bar` the given files declare => "Bar".
def kiosk_superclasses(paths)
  paths.each_with_object({}) do |path, acc|
    File.read(path).scan(/^\s*class\s+(Kiosk::[A-Za-z0-9_:]+)\s*<\s*([A-Za-z0-9_:]+)/) do |name, parent|
      acc[name] = parent
    end
  end
end

# The file's comments as one line, so a sentence that wraps across `#` lines
# still reads as a sentence. Ripper rather than a `^\s*#` scan: a `#` inside a
# string or a regex is not a comment, the same reason `code_digest` uses it.
def comment_prose(path)
  Ripper.lex(File.read(path))
        .select { |(_, type, _)| type == :on_comment }
        .map { |(_, _, tok)| tok.sub(/\A#+\s?/, "").rstrip }
        .join(" ")
        .gsub(/\s+/, " ")
end

APPLICATION_CONTROLLER = "app/controllers/application_controller.rb"

prose_units = demos.filter_map do |dir|
  ac = File.join(dir, APPLICATION_CONTROLLER)
  next unless File.exist?(ac)

  { label: short.(File.basename(dir)), ac: ac, rel: APPLICATION_CONTROLLER,
    where: "app/controllers/kiosk/",
    controllers: Dir[File.join(dir, "app/controllers/kiosk/**/*.rb")].sort }
end
e2e_ac = File.join(ROOT, "e2e/fixtures/application_controller.rb")
if File.exist?(e2e_ac)
  prose_units << { label: "e2e", ac: e2e_ac, rel: "e2e/fixtures/application_controller.rb",
                   where: "e2e/fixtures/",
                   controllers: Dir[File.join(ROOT, "e2e/fixtures/*_controller.rb")].sort - [e2e_ac] }
end

if prose_units.length < 2
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1021 prose rule found #{prose_units.length} " \
            "#{APPLICATION_CONTROLLER} copy — the file has moved, and a rule with nothing to " \
            "read passes everything."
end

prose_units.each do |unit|
  label, rel, where = unit[:label], unit[:rel], unit[:where]
  real = kiosk_superclasses(unit[:controllers])
  if real.empty?
    errors << "#{label}: ships #{rel} but no `class Kiosk::…Controller < …` could be read from " \
              "#{where} — the K-1021 prose rule would pass vacuously here. Fix the glob, do not " \
              "delete the rule."
    next
  end

  expected_super =
    real.values.uniq == ["ApplicationController"] ? "THIS class" : real.values.uniq.join("/")
  match = comment_prose(unit[:ac]).match(PROSE_SUPERCLASS_SENTENCE)
  unless match
    errors << "#{label}: #{rel} carries no checked superclass sentence. Its comments must say " \
              "«here #{real.keys.sort.join(' and ')} subclass #{expected_super}» (K-1021). This " \
              "file is compared `:code`, i.e. modulo comments, so that sentence is the ONLY thing " \
              "standing between the header and the eight-copy false claim K-1017 found."
    next
  end

  named = match[:names].scan(/Kiosk::[A-Za-z0-9_]+/).sort
  unless named == real.keys.sort
    errors << "#{label}: #{rel}'s comment names #{named.join(', ')} but #{where} declares " \
              "#{real.keys.sort.join(', ')} (K-1021). The prose is uncompared by the `:code` rule, " \
              "so nothing else notices."
  end

  claimed = match[:super] == "THIS class" ? "ApplicationController" : match[:super]
  actual  = real.values.uniq
  if actual.length > 1
    errors << "#{label}: #{rel}'s comment names ONE superclass (#{claimed}) but its wire " \
              "controllers declare #{real.map { |k, v| "#{k} < #{v}" }.sort.join(', ')} (K-1021). " \
              "Split the sentence, or make the superclasses agree."
  elsif actual.first != claimed
    errors << "#{label}: #{rel}'s comment says the wire controllers subclass #{claimed}, but they " \
              "declare `< #{actual.first}` (K-1021). kiosk-server ships a MIXIN and never a base " \
              "class, so this sentence is the app's own fact about its own tree — and it is " \
              "compared by nothing else, because the file's rule is `:code`."
  end
end

# ── The wrong-door hint must not name a RETIRED endpoint (K-1088) ────────────
#
# The `rescue_from ActionController::InvalidAuthenticityToken` body in these
# same files is not documentation: it is the LIVE JSON an assistant gets when it
# dials the human sign-in page — a reader that is by construction lost and has
# no way to check what it is told. Seven copies told it to fetch
# `/.well-known/kiosk.json` «for the register/login and schema/query/run/pay
# endpoints», which is false on both available readings: protocol 0.4 deleted
# `POST /kiosk/query` and `POST /kiosk/run` outright (T-074 = A — each demo's
# `routes.rb` says so, and all seven red-team batteries assert that a path
# naming no registered verb answers the plain 404), and `capabilities` has
# published MODULE names
# (schema/queries/actions/pay) since T-095. The `:code` rule held all seven
# identical — identically wrong — and `CHANGELOG.md`'s T-068 slice 7 had already
# corrected the same string on the human pages and left this one behind, which is
# the class: a sweep that fixes a file and not its sibling.
#
# Read from the CODE, comments stripped, so a comment may quote the retired
# string to explain why it went while a string literal may not. A blocklist is
# the right shape here and only here: these are names a protocol MAJOR removed,
# so the set changes at a protocol bump and nowhere else.
# The separator class is `[\s,/]+` and not a literal `/`: code spelled the list
# "schema/query/run/pay" and PROSE spells it "schema, query, run, pay", possibly
# across a line break. A pattern that knew only the slashes read the static pages
# as clean for a day (K-1090).
RETIRED_WIRE_NAMES = %r{schema[\s,/]+quer(?:y|ies)[\s,/]+run[\s,/]+pay|/kiosk/(?:query|run)\b}

# The file with its comments removed. Ripper rather than a `^\s*#` scan, for the
# reason `code_digest` and `comment_prose` both give: a `#` inside a string or a
# regex is not a comment.
def code_source(path)
  Ripper.lex(File.read(path))
        .reject { |(_, type, _)| COMMENT_TOKENS.include?(type) }
        .map { |(_, _, tok)| tok }
        .join
end

prose_units.each do |unit|
  hits = code_source(unit[:ac]).scan(RETIRED_WIRE_NAMES).uniq
  next if hits.empty?

  errors << "#{unit[:label]}: #{unit[:rel]} names #{hits.map(&:inspect).join(', ')} in CODE — the " \
            "0.3 wire vocabulary protocol 0.4 deleted (K-1088). NO route is drawn for `query` " \
            "or `run` (T-074 = A), and no catch-all stands behind them: both names match " \
            "nothing and answer the ordinary 404 any undrawn path gets (K-1112). " \
            "`capabilities` publishes MODULE names " \
            "(schema/queries/actions/pay) since T-095, so this string is false however a reader " \
            "takes it — and it is a LIVE response body, not a comment. Name what " \
            "/.well-known/kiosk.json carries (the register/login endpoints, the catalog link, the " \
            "modules this origin serves); do NOT re-enumerate verbs, which would be a third copy " \
            "of the catalog kiosk.json deliberately stopped publishing."
end

# ── The SAME ban on the THREE STATIC PAGES, which is where it survived (K-1090)
#
# The three `public/*.html` pages are held byte-identical across the operator
# seven a few hundred lines above — and byte-identity only guarantees the seven
# copies are WRONG TOGETHER. The check above reads the CSRF rescue body and
# stops there, so the identical enumeration K-1088 deleted from that body stayed
# LIVE in `public/404.html` of all seven: «Fetch /.well-known/kiosk.json for the
# endpoints it actually serves — register/login, schema, query, run, pay».
#
# That page is the one with the wider reach of the two: PublicExceptions serves
# it for EVERY unrouted path, which is why this file's own `why:` for it calls it
# «the answer an assistant guessing web-app paths hits most often», where the
# CSRF body is reached only after a JSON-dialing caller has already hit a human
# form. And the class had ALREADY been swept once — `CHANGELOG.md`'s T-068 slice
# 7 corrected 422.html and 500.html and left 404.html behind — so this is the
# third survivor of one class. A check that reads only Ruby cannot see that.
#
# Two retired vocabularies are refused here, both deleted by a protocol MAJOR:
#
#   * RETIRED_WIRE_NAMES — the 0.3 verb list, for the reason stated above it.
#   * RETIRED_ERROR_SHAPE — the word «envelope». 0.4 answers a FLAT RFC 9457
#     problem document served as `application/problem+json`, whose top-level
#     `code` is the branch point (K-808, T-074 = A); «envelope» is the word the
#     deleted `{ok:false, error:{…}}` shape owned. The engine's own auth.md was
#     corrected by T-068 slice 7 and kiosk-server's suite PINS the phrase out
#     (`well_known_spec.rb`: `expect(body).not_to match(/JSON envelope/)`) — all
#     three static pages kept it, so they described RFC 9457 in the vocabulary of
#     the thing RFC 9457 replaced, on the surface least able to check.
#
# THE WHOLE FILE IS JUDGED, COMMENTS INCLUDED — and that is the one place this
# rule differs from the Ruby one above, which strips comments so a comment may
# quote the retired string to explain why it went. It cannot here: an HTML
# comment in a static page IS served, byte for byte, to whoever fetched the page.
# MEASURED — `curl` at an unrouted path on a booted demo returns the header
# comment along with the body, so a comment quoting the dead verb list would hand
# that list to the very reader the fix exists to protect. The header comments on
# these three pages therefore describe what the sentence used to say WITHOUT
# reproducing it. prove is scanned too — its pages are the declared exception to
# byte-IDENTITY, never an exception to what is true.
STATIC_SIGNPOST_PAGES = %w[public/404.html public/422.html public/500.html].freeze
RETIRED_ERROR_SHAPE = /envelope/i

static_pages = 0
demos.each do |dir|
  label = short.(File.basename(dir))
  STATIC_SIGNPOST_PAGES.each do |rel|
    path = File.join(dir, rel)
    next unless File.exist?(path)

    static_pages += 1
    body = File.read(path)
    hits = (body.scan(RETIRED_WIRE_NAMES) + body.scan(RETIRED_ERROR_SHAPE)).uniq
    next if hits.empty?

    errors << "#{label}: #{rel} names #{hits.map(&:inspect).join(', ')} in the file, which is SERVED whole — " \
              "retired 0.3 vocabulary (K-1090). NO route is drawn for `query` or `run` " \
              "(T-074 = A), and no catch-all stands behind them: both names match nothing and " \
              "answer the ordinary 404 any undrawn path gets (K-1112). " \
              "`capabilities` publishes MODULE names since T-095, and an " \
              "error is a FLAT RFC 9457 problem document under `application/problem+json`, not " \
              "an «envelope» (K-808). PublicExceptions serves this page to every assistant that " \
              "guesses a path, so it cannot check what it is told. Name what " \
              "/.well-known/kiosk.json carries (the register/login endpoints, the catalog link, " \
              "the modules this origin serves) and enumerate no verbs; say " \
              "`application/problem+json` and point at the top-level `code`."
  end
end

expected_static = demos.size * STATIC_SIGNPOST_PAGES.size
if static_pages != expected_static
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1090 static-page rule read #{static_pages} of " \
            "#{expected_static} pages (#{demos.size} demos x #{STATIC_SIGNPOST_PAGES.size}) — a " \
            "demo is missing one of #{STATIC_SIGNPOST_PAGES.join(', ')}, which is the K-532 " \
            "bodyless-error class itself, and a rule with nothing to read passes everything."
end

# ── A deploy/env template may not cite a path that is not there (K-1093) ─────
#
# Six templates told an operator the PoW difficulty knob was «wired via
# `lib/pow_difficulty.rb`». No demo has ever had a file at that path: it was
# `app/services/pow_difficulty.rb` in all seven until T-189 promoted the module
# into kiosk-pow-equihash, and `deploy/README.md` — the
# runbook shipped in the same directory — said so correctly the whole time. So
# the two disagreed about a path, and the templates are the file an operator
# actually opens while editing values. The stale bootsnap caches still on disk
# in each demo's `tmp/` name the OLD location, which is what this was: a cite
# that was true once and rotted silently when the file moved. It is the SECOND
# time a `deploy/` document has been found citing a path nobody verified
# (`CHANGELOG.md`, 2026-07-21, same directory), which is what makes it a rule
# rather than a fix.
#
# `skooti.env.example` is the shape to PREFER and needs no rule at all — it
# names the constant (`registration_pow_params =
# Kiosk::Pow::Equihash::Difficulty.params`), and a
# constant does not rot when a file moves. But a template that DOES cite a path
# must cite one that exists in the demo it configures, which is derived from the
# template's own filename rather than listed here. Only cites containing a `/`
# are read: a bare `production.rb` names a file without saying where it lives,
# and this rule will not guess for it.
ENV_TEMPLATE_DIR  = "deploy/env"
ENV_TEMPLATE_CITE = %r{(?<![\w./-])(?!/)[a-z][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*(?:/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)+\.(?:rb|yml|yaml|erb)\b}

env_templates = 0
env_cites     = 0
Dir[File.join(ROOT, ENV_TEMPLATE_DIR, "*.env.example")].sort.each do |tpl|
  stem = File.basename(tpl, ".env.example")
  demo = File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-#{stem}")
  # kyc-demo.env.example configures the prove broker under a name of its own and
  # has no `kiosk-demo-<stem>` to resolve against; skipping it is deliberate and
  # is why the count below is asserted rather than assumed.
  next unless File.directory?(demo)

  env_templates += 1
  File.readlines(tpl).each_with_index do |line, i|
    line.scan(ENV_TEMPLATE_CITE).each do |cite|
      env_cites += 1
      next if File.exist?(File.join(demo, cite))

      errors << "#{ENV_TEMPLATE_DIR}/#{File.basename(tpl)}:#{i + 1}: cites `#{cite}`, which does " \
                "not exist in kiosk-demo-#{stem} (K-1093). A shipped template is what an operator " \
                "opens while editing values, so a path in it is followed — name the CONSTANT " \
                "instead, the way skooti.env.example does, since a constant does not rot when a " \
                "file moves; or cite the path the demo actually has."
    end
  end
end

if env_templates.zero? || env_cites.zero?
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1093 template-cite rule read #{env_templates} template(s) " \
            "and #{env_cites} path cite(s) — either #{ENV_TEMPLATE_DIR}/ moved, the templates no " \
            "longer share a stem with their demo directory, or the cite pattern stopped matching. " \
            "A rule that inspects nothing passes everything."
end

# ── The flow-runner helper: lib/tasks/ is not compared, this construct is (K-1046)
#
# `lib/tasks/*.rake` sits outside SCAN_ROOTS, and the reason recorded up there
# was true when it was written: a demo's rake file IS per-demo, task by task.
# K-1043 made half of it false in one wave. Closing the flow harness's fail-open
# — read the child's exit status before parsing its JSON — needed the same fifty
# lines of comment and `def` in every demo, so the fleet now carries a construct
# that is byte-identical in SIX rake files and load-bearing in SEVEN, in the one
# directory nothing compares. bin/check-ci-tasks is not cover: it compares the
# task INVENTORY (ci.yml against the tasks each demo defines, plus the README
# table it generates), and never one demo's rake file against another's. So the
# drift argument this whole check opens with applied verbatim, and to the one
# construct every operator demo's ability to FAIL now rests on.
#
# The FRAGMENT is declared rather than the file, because the rest of the rake
# file genuinely is per-demo. Each demo names its helper after itself
# (`hoteling_run_flow`): a rake file's top-level `def` lands on Object, so a
# generic `run_flow` would be a fleet-wide name defined in a per-demo file. The
# name is normalised away before comparing, so that prefix stays a convention
# rather than a hole.
#
# TWO THINGS ARE COMPARED, because tudu's helper is deliberately not a copy:
#
#   1. THE WHOLE BLOCK — the contiguous comment header plus the `def`, byte-for
#      byte modulo the helper's name — across every demo not declared a variant.
#      Prose included, deliberately: that header is the argument for why the
#      status is read at all, and `:code` would leave it exactly as unguarded as
#      the sentence K-1017 found repeated, falsely, in eight copies at once.
#   2. THE TAIL — from the `json_line` assignment to the end of the def —
#      across EVERY demo, variants included. That is the part that decides
#      whether a task can fail at all: status read FIRST, abort quoting the
#      child's own last output line, JSON parsed only after. It is the same
#      nineteen lines in all seven. A variant may differ in how it BUILDS the
#      child (tudu takes a server URL and an env hash and calls Open3 in ARGV
#      form, with no shell); it may not differ in what it does with the child's
#      status, and a rule that exempted the variant outright would leave the
#      seventh copy of exactly that code guarded by nothing.
#
# `ships:` is the same declaration the COPIES entries carry, for the same reason
# (K-860, K-883): a demo that grows a helper without being listed fails here
# rather than becoming an eighth copy nobody reasoned about, and a listed demo
# that loses one fails too.
FLOW_RUNNER_REL   = "lib/tasks/demo.rake"
FLOW_RUNNER_SHIPS = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling philslist skooti stylish tudu].freeze
FLOW_RUNNER_TAIL  = /^\s*json_line\s*=/
FLOW_RUNNER_VARIANTS = {
  "tudu" => "tudu's four flow tasks pass a server URL and an extra-env hash rather than a shell " \
            "env string, so its helper is `(flow_rb, server_url, extra_env)` and calls " \
            "Open3.capture2e in ARGV form — no shell, nothing to escape, and its header prose " \
            "says so in its own words. That is a real difference in how the child is BUILT, not " \
            "drift, and unifying it is explicitly out of scope (K-1046). What the child's status " \
            "then buys is compared anyway, by the tail rule.",
}.freeze

# The helper's comment header + `def`, with the demo-specific name normalised
# away. A rake file's helper is a top-level `def`, so the block runs from the
# contiguous `#` lines above it down to the first column-0 `end`.
def flow_runner_block(path, helper)
  lines = File.readlines(path)
  di = lines.index { |l| l.start_with?("def #{helper}(") } or return nil
  ei = (di...lines.size).find { |i| lines[i] == "end\n" } or return nil

  si = di
  si -= 1 while si.positive? && lines[si - 1].start_with?("#")
  lines[si..ei].join.gsub(helper, "run_flow")
end

flow_runner_helpers = demos.filter_map do |dir|
  demo = short.(File.basename(dir))
  path = File.join(dir, FLOW_RUNNER_REL)
  next unless File.exist?(path)
  next unless File.read(path).include?("def #{demo}_run_flow(")

  [demo, path]
end.to_h

flow_stray = flow_runner_helpers.keys - FLOW_RUNNER_SHIPS
flow_gone  = FLOW_RUNNER_SHIPS - flow_runner_helpers.keys
unless flow_stray.empty?
  errors << "#{flow_stray.sort.join(', ')}: #{FLOW_RUNNER_REL} defines a `<demo>_run_flow` helper " \
            "but #{flow_stray.size == 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} not in the flow-runner rule's declared " \
            "`ships:` set (#{FLOW_RUNNER_SHIPS.join(', ')}). A copy of this helper is not free — " \
            "add the demo to FLOW_RUNNER_SHIPS in bin/check-demo-copies so its copy is held in " \
            "lockstep with the others (K-1046)."
end
unless flow_gone.empty?
  errors << "#{flow_gone.sort.join(', ')}: the flow-runner rule names #{flow_gone.size == 1 ? 'it' : 'them'} " \
            "in FLOW_RUNNER_SHIPS, but #{FLOW_RUNNER_REL} there defines no `<demo>_run_flow` any " \
            "more. Either the demo stopped driving flows — update the declared set — or its tasks " \
            "went back to capturing a child without reading its exit status, which is the K-1043 " \
            "fail-open this helper exists to close."
end

flow_runner_blocks = {}
flow_runner_helpers.each do |demo, path|
  block = flow_runner_block(path, "#{demo}_run_flow")
  if block.nil? || Ripper.sexp(block).nil?
    errors << "#{demo}: #{FLOW_RUNNER_REL} defines #{demo}_run_flow but its comment+`def` block " \
              "could not be read back as valid Ruby — the helper is no longer a top-level `def` " \
              "closed by a column-0 `end`, so the flow-runner rule compared NOTHING for this demo. " \
              "Fix the extraction in bin/check-demo-copies, do not drop the rule (K-1046)."
    next
  end
  flow_runner_blocks[demo] = block
end

if flow_runner_blocks.size < 2
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1046 flow-runner rule read #{flow_runner_blocks.size} " \
            "helper#{flow_runner_blocks.size == 1 ? '' : 's'} — the construct has moved out of " \
            "#{FLOW_RUNNER_REL}, and a lockstep rule with nothing to compare passes everything."
end

FLOW_RUNNER_VARIANTS.each do |demo, why|
  errors << "#{demo}: flow-runner `variants:` reason is blank" if why.to_s.strip.empty?
  next if flow_runner_blocks.key?(demo)

  errors << "#{demo}: declared a flow-runner variant in bin/check-demo-copies, but that demo " \
            "defines no #{demo}_run_flow — a variant declaration for a helper that is not there " \
            "is the record turning back into an assumption. Drop the entry (K-1046)."
end

flow_held = flow_runner_blocks.reject { |demo, _| FLOW_RUNNER_VARIANTS.key?(demo) }
flow_groups = flow_held.group_by { |_demo, block| block }
if flow_groups.size > 1
  listed = flow_groups.values.map { |g| "[#{g.map { |d, _| d }.sort.join(',')}]" }.sort.join(" ")
  errors << "#{FLOW_RUNNER_REL}: the `<demo>_run_flow` helpers must agree byte-for-byte once the " \
            "helper's own name is substituted, and they do not: #{listed}. This block is " \
            "hand-copied into every demo — propagate the edit to all of them, or, if one copy " \
            "legitimately differs, declare it in FLOW_RUNNER_VARIANTS with the reason (K-1046)."
elsif flow_groups.size == 1
  canonical = flow_groups.keys.first
  FLOW_RUNNER_VARIANTS.each_key do |demo|
    next unless flow_runner_blocks[demo] == canonical

    errors << "#{demo}: flow-runner `variants:` entry is stale — its helper now matches the other " \
              "copies byte-for-byte. Drop the entry so the block is held in lockstep like the rest."
  end
end

flow_tails = {}
flow_runner_blocks.each do |demo, block|
  ti = block.lines.index { |l| l.match?(FLOW_RUNNER_TAIL) }
  if ti.nil?
    errors << "#{demo}: #{demo}_run_flow contains no `json_line = ` assignment, so the tail the " \
              "flow-runner rule exists to compare — status read first, abort quoting the child's " \
              "last line, JSON parsed only after — was read as NOTHING here (K-1046). Either the " \
              "helper was rewritten, in which case say so and re-anchor the rule, or it lost the " \
              "K-1043 fix."
    next
  end
  flow_tails[demo] = block.lines[ti..].join
end

flow_tail_groups = flow_tails.group_by { |_demo, tail| tail }
if flow_tail_groups.size > 1
  listed = flow_tail_groups.values.map { |g| "[#{g.map { |d, _| d }.sort.join(',')}]" }.sort.join(" ")
  errors << "#{FLOW_RUNNER_REL}: every `<demo>_run_flow` — variants included — must handle the " \
            "child's exit status with the SAME lines, and they do not: #{listed}. A helper may " \
            "differ in how it builds the child; what it does with the child's status is the one " \
            "thing K-1043 closed, and it is the reason a task can fail at all (K-1046)."
end

# ── The host-detection block: lib/tasks/ is not compared, this construct is (K-1353)
#
# THE SECOND construct in `lib/tasks/*.rake`, and it is here for the reason the
# flow-runner rule above is: that directory sits outside SCAN_ROOTS because a
# demo's rake file IS per-demo task by task, and a construct hand-copied across
# four of them is nevertheless a hand-copied construct with nothing watching it.
# The demos in HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS below boot their servers on a vanity
# `<demo>.demo.kiosk.tech` host, and every task that boots one opens with the
# same block: an OPT-IN resolver
# lookup (KIOSK_DEMO_HOST_LOOKUP unset means no outbound DNS query at all), a
# `rescue StandardError` that is deliberately wider than `Resolv::ResolvError`
# because `Resolv::ResolvTimeout` is not one of those, and the 127.0.0.1
# fallback with the /etc/hosts hint an operator needs to reach the vanity name.
#
# WHAT IT LOOKED LIKE WHEN THIS RULE WAS WRITTEN, and why a scan-root change was
# not the answer. MEASURED by extracting all twenty-five blocks, substituting the
# demo's own name and grouping: FOUR distinct forms — thirteen a silent ternary,
# six printing the operator hint with an em dash, four with a double hyphen, two
# without the parentheses. Whether the run told an operator how to reach the
# vanity host depended on which task they happened to run, which is an accident
# rather than a decision. The obvious homes were both measured wrong before this
# one was written: adding `lib/tasks/` to SCAN_ROOTS is a NO-OP, because the scan
# globs `**/*.rb` and `kiosk-demo-*/lib/tasks/**/*.rb` matches zero files; and
# teaching that glob `.rake` would then whole-file-compare eight rake files that
# are per-demo by construction, which is the signal-drowning this file's own
# header declines in writing. So: the construct is declared, and only it.
#
# WHAT IS COMPARED, AND WHY IT IS THE BLOCK'S MEANING RATHER THAN ITS BYTES.
# Every block is read from its `host = begin` to the `end` at the same
# indentation, the demo's OWN vanity host is substituted for a placeholder, and
# what is left must agree across every site in every demo that ships one. The
# substitution is the whole of the difference between bytes and meaning here:
# the host name is the one thing that legitimately varies, and normalising it is
# what makes «these four demos detect their host the same way» a checkable claim
# instead of a diff. It also means a block naming a SIBLING's host — hoteling's
# copy reaching for skooti's name — is red, because the substitution cannot
# cancel a name that is not this demo's.
#
# NO `variants:` MAP, deliberately, and that is a difference from the flow-runner
# rule rather than an oversight: tudu's flow helper is a genuine variant because
# it builds the child process differently, whereas there is no way for one demo
# to legitimately detect its own host differently from another. If one ever needs
# to, the map is the change to make and the reason is the thing to write down.
#
# `ships:` is the same declaration the COPIES entries carry, for the same reason
# (K-860, K-883): a demo that grows a host-detecting task without being listed
# fails here rather than becoming another copy nobody reasoned about, and a
# listed demo that loses its last one fails too.
#
# `why:` FOR THE SET ITSELF, WHICH IT DID NOT CARRY (K-1379), AND THE SET IS NOW
# EVERY OPERATOR DEMO THAT BOOTS A SERVER (K-1400). Every COPIES entry states a
# reason; this one named four demos and said nothing about the three it left out,
# so a reader could not tell a decision from a half-finished sweep. MEASURED at
# head then, and the answer was: NOBODY DECIDED IT.
#
#   * philslist, stylish and tudu are operator demos that boot servers and have a
#     deployed vanity host in `deploy/README.md` (`philslist.demo.kiosk.tech`,
#     `stylish.demo.kiosk.tech`, `tudu.demo.kiosk.tech`, ports 3005-3007), and
#     they shipped NO host-detection block: `command grep -c "host = begin"` over
#     each one's `lib/tasks/demo.rake` -> 0, against 8 / 6 / 6 / 5 for the four
#     listed. Their tasks hard-coded `http://127.0.0.1:#{port}` and therefore
#     printed no hint that a vanity name existed.
#   * There is nothing about them that makes the block inapplicable. It is
#     opt-in on `KIOSK_DEMO_HOST_LOOKUP=1`, makes no outbound query otherwise,
#     and falls back to loopback -- which is what those three did unconditionally.
#   * The set was EXACTLY the four demos that carry a published `before-after.md`
#     recording (`BEFORE_AFTER_FOUR` below is the same four names, arrived at
#     independently). That is a correlation with the DOCUMENTS, not with any
#     capability, and it was not chronological either: stylish predates two of the
#     four. No ADR and no ledger row recorded a choice; K-1318 and K-1353 both
#     swept «all twenty-five blocks» and neither asked about the demos with none.
#
# THE DECISION, taken because the `why:` above could name no reason for the line
# it drew: the block is now in all seven operator demos, sixteen further sites,
# and `HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS` is the seven. WHAT AN OPERATOR'S RUN PRINTS CHANGED, and
# that is the whole visible effect: a task in philslist, stylish or tudu that used
# to say nothing now prints, once, «(using 127.0.0.1 — to reach
# <demo>.demo.kiosk.tech instead, add it to /etc/hosts as 127.0.0.1 and set
# KIOSK_DEMO_HOST_LOOKUP=1)», and with that env var AND an /etc/hosts entry it
# dials the vanity name instead of loopback. Nothing else moves: the lookup stays
# opt-in so no run gains an outbound DNS query, the server is still BOUND to
# 127.0.0.1 in every demo, and only the URL the harness dials (hence the `Host`
# header) follows the name. The three demos' `config/environments/development.rb`
# gained the `config.hosts <<` entry the block's own comment requires, without
# which the printed /etc/hosts line would earn a Rails 8 HostAuthorization 403.
# `kiosk-demo-prove` is deliberately NOT in the set: it is an ISSUER, not a Kiosk
# operator, its rake file boots no server of its own, and a sibling boots it.
HOST_BLOCK_REL   = "lib/tasks/demo.rake"
HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling philslist skooti stylish tudu].freeze
HOST_BLOCK_OPEN  = /\A(\s*)host = begin\s*\z/
HOST_BLOCK_NAME  = "<demo>.demo.kiosk.tech"

# Every host-detection block in one demo's rake file, as [line, normalised text].
# `nil` means the extractor lost its footing — an opener with no `end` at its own
# indentation — which is reported rather than silently read as «no blocks».
def host_detection_blocks(path, demo)
  lines = File.readlines(path)
  out   = []
  lines.each_with_index do |line, i|
    m = HOST_BLOCK_OPEN.match(line) or next

    indent = m[1]
    j      = i + 1
    j += 1 while j < lines.size && lines[j] != "#{indent}end\n"
    return nil if j >= lines.size

    out << [i + 1, lines[i..j].join.gsub("#{demo}.demo.kiosk.tech", HOST_BLOCK_NAME)]
  end
  out
end

host_block_sites   = 0
host_blocks        = {}   # demo => [[line, text], ...]
host_block_broken  = []
demos.each do |dir|
  demo = short.(File.basename(dir))
  path = File.join(dir, HOST_BLOCK_REL)
  next unless File.exist?(path)

  found = host_detection_blocks(path, demo)
  if found.nil?
    host_block_broken << demo
    next
  end
  next if found.empty?

  host_blocks[demo] = found
  host_block_sites += found.size
end

unless host_block_broken.empty?
  errors << "#{host_block_broken.sort.join(', ')}: #{HOST_BLOCK_REL} opens a `host = begin` block " \
            "the extractor could not close — no `end` at the opener's own indentation — so the " \
            "host-detection rule compared NOTHING for that demo. Fix the extraction in " \
            "bin/check-demo-copies, do not drop the rule (K-1353)."
end

host_stray = host_blocks.keys - HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS
host_gone  = HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS - host_blocks.keys
unless host_stray.empty?
  errors << "#{host_stray.sort.join(', ')}: #{HOST_BLOCK_REL} detects a demo host but " \
            "#{host_stray.size == 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} not in the host-detection rule's declared " \
            "`ships:` set (#{HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS.join(', ')}). A copy of this block is not free — add " \
            "the demo to HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS in bin/check-demo-copies so its copies are held in " \
            "lockstep with the others (K-1353)."
end
unless host_gone.empty?
  errors << "#{host_gone.sort.join(', ')}: the host-detection rule names #{host_gone.size == 1 ? 'it' : 'them'} " \
            "in HOST_BLOCK_SHIPS, but #{HOST_BLOCK_REL} there detects no demo host any more. Either " \
            "that demo stopped booting a server on its vanity name — update the declared set — or " \
            "the block was lost and its tasks now dial whatever the last edit left behind (K-1353)."
end

if host_block_sites < 2
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1353 host-detection rule read #{host_block_sites} " \
            "block#{host_block_sites == 1 ? '' : 's'} — the construct has moved out of " \
            "#{HOST_BLOCK_REL}, and a lockstep rule with nothing to compare passes everything."
end

host_flat = host_blocks.flat_map { |demo, found| found.map { |line, text| ["#{demo}:#{line}", text] } }
host_groups = host_flat.group_by { |_where, text| text }
if host_groups.size > 1
  listed = host_groups.values.map { |g| "[#{g.map { |w, _| w }.sort.join(',')}]" }.sort.join(" ")
  errors << "#{HOST_BLOCK_REL}: every `host = begin` block must agree once the demo's own vanity " \
            "host is substituted, and they do not: #{listed}. This block is hand-copied into every " \
            "task that boots a server — propagate the edit to all of them. Whether the run tells " \
            "an operator how to reach the vanity host may not depend on which task they ran " \
            "(K-1353)."
end

# ── Every task that boots a server detects its host (K-1413) ─────────────────
#
# THE RULE ABOVE HOLDS THE BLOCKS THAT EXIST AND CANNOT SEE ONE GO MISSING.
# Its two halves are «every block that is there must agree after substitution»
# and «a declared demo must have at least one». MEASURED (K-1413): replacing ONE
# of stylish's six blocks with a hard-coded loopback literal left the guard at
# exit 0 — the other five still agreed with everyone else's, and the demo still
# had blocks, so both halves passed while one task quietly stopped telling an
# operator the vanity name existed. K-1400 then took the guarded population from
# twenty-five sites in four demos to forty-one in seven, which widened the blind
# spot rather than closing it.
#
# WHAT CLOSES IT IS A PAIRING, NOT A COUNT. A per-demo tally would be a
# hand-kept number — the thing K-863 and K-1379 both record going stale — so the
# rule is derived from the file instead: the rake file is cut at every `task`
# declaration, and in each task the number of `host = begin` blocks must be 1 if
# that task boots a server and 0 if it does not. Losing one block leaves a
# booting task with none, which is red; adding a booting task without one is red
# for the same reason and needs no edit here; and a block left behind in a task
# that no longer boots is red too, because a resolver lookup nothing dials is a
# lie about what the run does.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS BOOTING, and why it is two spellings rather than one. Five
# demos spawn `bundle exec rails s` inline (hoteling and skooti wrap theirs in a
# task-local `boot_server` lambda called two or three times, which is still ONE
# spawn site and ONE host block). philslist and tudu hoist the spawn into a
# file-level `<demo>_boot_server` helper that takes the host as a KEYWORD, so
# their tasks boot by calling it. The helper itself lives before the first task
# and is deliberately outside the pairing: it has no host to detect, it is
# handed one.
#
# TWO TASKS FAILED THIS THE DAY IT WAS WRITTEN and were fixed rather than
# excepted: getgrocery's `demo:pow` and hoteling's `demo:browse` both spawned a
# server and hard-coded `http://127.0.0.1:#{port}`, so K-1400's sweep — whose
# own prose says «every task that boots one opens with the same block» — had
# missed them, and nothing could have said so. There is no exemption list here
# on purpose: an exemption is the hand-kept thing this rule exists to avoid.
HOST_PAIR_BOOT = "bundle exec rails s "
HOST_PAIR_TASK = /\A\s*task[\s(:]/

# One entry per rake task: [line, declaration, boot sites, host blocks].
def host_pair_tasks(path, demo)
  lines  = File.readlines(path)
  starts = lines.each_index.select { |i| lines[i] =~ HOST_PAIR_TASK }
  starts.each_with_index.map do |a, k|
    body  = lines[a..((starts[k + 1] || lines.size) - 1)]
    boots = body.count { |l| l.include?(HOST_PAIR_BOOT) } +
            body.count { |l| l.include?("#{demo}_boot_server(") && l !~ /\A\s*def\s/ }
    [a + 1, lines[a].strip, boots, body.count { |l| HOST_BLOCK_OPEN.match?(l) }]
  end
end

host_pair_booting = 0
host_pair_bad     = []
demos.each do |dir|
  demo = short.(File.basename(dir))
  path = File.join(dir, HOST_BLOCK_REL)
  next unless File.exist?(path)

  host_pair_tasks(path, demo).each do |line, decl, boots, blocks|
    next if boots.zero? && blocks.zero?

    host_pair_booting += 1 if boots.positive?
    want = boots.positive? ? 1 : 0
    next if blocks == want

    host_pair_bad << "#{demo}:#{line} `#{decl}` boots #{boots} server#{boots == 1 ? '' : 's'} " \
                     "and detects its host #{blocks} time#{blocks == 1 ? '' : 's'}"
  end
end

unless host_pair_bad.empty?
  errors << "#{HOST_BLOCK_REL}: every task that boots a server must carry exactly one " \
            "`host = begin` block, and a task that boots none may not carry one — " \
            "#{host_pair_bad.sort.join('; ')}. The lockstep rule above compares the blocks that " \
            "EXIST and so cannot see one go missing: a task that loses its block keeps dialling " \
            "whatever literal the edit left behind and stops printing the /etc/hosts hint, while " \
            "its siblings still agree with each other (K-1413)."
end

if host_pair_booting < 2
  errors << "check-demo-copies: the K-1413 host-pairing rule found #{host_pair_booting} " \
            "server-booting task#{host_pair_booting == 1 ? '' : 's'} — the boot construct has moved " \
            "out of #{HOST_BLOCK_REL}, and a pairing rule with nothing paired passes everything."
end

# ── A shared UNIT inside a `:per_demo` file (T-120) ──────────────────────────
#
# The lockstep rule's handle is a relative PATH, so a method shared by two demos
# inside a file the manifest calls `:per_demo` — which this file's own header
# defines as «Not compared» — sits BELOW its granularity and is compared to
# nothing. That is not hypothetical and it is not new: `whole_number/1` is
# byte-identical in atablefor and getgrocery (K-1027/K-1020) and `missing/1` in
# getgrocery, hoteling and skooti, five hand-copies across two methods, all
# inside `app/operations/wire_arguments.rb`, which is `:per_demo` for a good
# reason — the guards ARE each demo's argument vocabulary, and sharing the FILE
# would mean shipping every demo guards for arguments it does not take.
#
# WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT THE OTHER TWO T-120 NAMED.
#
#   * PROMOTING the shared half into the gem the fleet already reaches for
#     (Phil's rule, T-089/K-792) is the right answer for a thing with a public
#     contract, and the wrong one here: `whole_number` is a four-line predicate
#     and `missing` is a three-line sentence, and putting either behind a gem
#     boundary buys a versioned public API, a CHANGELOG entry and a release for
#     each future word of a refusal message — while the copies stop being
#     readable next to the guard that calls them, which is most of their value.
#     The promotions this file has seen went the other way round: `UuidCheck`
#     and `PowDifficulty` moved into a gem because each has a contract two
#     parties read. A four-line predicate has one caller and no contract, and
#     the restraint applies twice as hard one level down.
#   * DECLARING the set as a known duplicate with a reason buys detection of a
#     THIRD copy and nothing else — the one-sided edit, which is the entire
#     class this script exists for, stays exactly as undetectable as it was.
#
# So the unit is DECLARED and COMPARED, the way the K-1046 flow-runner rule
# already declares and compares a fragment inside another uncompared file. Two
# things it takes from that rule and one it does not:
#
#   * TAKES the `ships:` declaration (K-860, K-883) and the two-directional
#     staleness check, so a demo growing a copy fails rather than becoming a
#     fourth copy nobody reasoned about, and a demo losing one fails too.
#   * TAKES, since K-1263, the file-level manifest's `except:` map — «one copy
#     that legitimately differs from its siblings», the K-643 escape hatch,
#     one level down. WITHOUT IT THE MANIFEST COULD NOT STATE A TRUE THING:
#     `app/models/user.rb#devise` is `devise :database_authenticatable` in six
#     demos and `devise :database_authenticatable, :registerable` in tudu, the
#     one demo whose humans sign themselves up. `:code` failed on tudu, and
#     pinning `ships:` to the six failed on tudu as a stray, because discovery
#     requires every copy of a shared unit to be in its entry's set — so the
#     only declarable rule was `:per_demo`, and SIX copies of the line that
#     decides which Devise modules an operator's human IdP loads were compared
#     to nothing. The exception is DECLARED with what makes that copy
#     different, and it is checked exactly as hard as the file-level one: a
#     reason may not be blank, an exception for a demo that no longer defines
#     the unit is stale, an exception whose copy has since CONVERGED on its
#     siblings is stale, and an entry where every copy but one is excepted
#     guards nothing and says so. A seventh demo growing a divergent `devise`
#     line still reddens, because it is compared, not excepted.
#   * TAKES the loud-failure-on-unreadable rule: a unit the extractor cannot
#     read back as valid Ruby is an ERROR, never a silent skip, because a
#     lockstep rule with nothing to compare passes everything.
#   * DOES NOT take «compare the prose too». The flow-runner block is ONE
#     construct with ONE argument written above it, so its header is part of
#     what is copied. These units are the opposite: each copy's prose is that
#     demo's own reasoning about its own arguments — atablefor's `whole_number`
#     explains a party, getgrocery's a quantity, and both are correct. So the
#     comparison is `:code`, the manifest's own «identical once comments and
#     whitespace are normalised away», applied one level down through the same
#     {code_norm}.
#
# DISCOVERY, not just declaration, and that is the half that makes this a check
# rather than a list: every unit name that appears in TWO OR MORE copies of a
# declared path must be in FRAGMENTS. A new shared method cannot appear
# silently, which is the property the file-level manifest has and the reason six
# of those copies were found at all. It is also why `priceable_total` is
# declared `:per_demo` below rather than left out — getgrocery's takes a total,
# hoteling's takes a total AND the nights it spans, and a shared NAME with
# different bodies is a thing a reader should be told about once rather than
# discover by diffing.
#
# WHICH PATHS ARE IN, AND THE RULE THE NEXT ONE IS JUDGED BY (T-136, T-145).
# Seventeen manifest paths are `:per_demo`. Running the extractor below over all
# seventeen at head finds **124 unit NAMES shared by two or more copies**, of
# which **76** also have identical bodies once comments and whitespace are
# normalised away. Both readings are stated because they answer different
# questions: discovery fires on a shared NAME whatever the bodies do, and only
# the narrower number is what a lockstep rule can actually hold. Re-measured for
# T-136 with the extractor above — the 100/38 this paragraph carried before is
# not reproducible at head under either reading, nor under a third (55) that also
# requires the unit to exist in every copy of its path; the tree moved, and the
# extractor stopped dropping a name's second definition.
#
# THE FIRST QUESTION WAS NOT «WHICH UNITS» BUT «IS THE MANIFEST ENTRY RIGHT»,
# because a fragment rule on a path that should have been compared WHOLE
# entrenches the wrong granularity — and the rule below deliberately errors on a
# `:code` path for exactly that reason. MEASURED, and the answer is no for the
# two entries that looked likeliest: `script/pay_window.rb` normalises to 194
# lines in hoteling and 181 in skooti with only 100 in common (a 198-line
# unified diff), and `script/binding_flow.rb` to 69 / 125 / 119 lines across
# atablefor / philslist / stylish. Their UNITS agree; their beats — the
# fixtures, the verbs driven, the terminal assertion — do not, which is what
# those `:per_demo` entries already say. The entries stand.
#
# THE CRITERION FOR TAKING A PATH, applied to each one below and written down so
# the next one is judged the same way: take it when every unit shared by two or
# more copies is either identical, or differs for a reason that belongs to the
# DEMO — its own vocabulary, its own fixture, its own operator identity. Leave it
# when a shared name differs only by HARNESS GENERATION, because a `:per_demo`
# fragment entry would then record an accident as a decision, and the convergence
# that ought to happen instead (T-119) would arrive as a red build demanding a
# dozen re-declarations.
#
# TAKEN — every `:per_demo` path that passes it, which is FOURTEEN of the
# seventeen and 88 of the 124 shared names. Shared/identical at head:
# `script/binding_flow.rb` 16/16, `script/pay_window.rb` 19/15 (the four that
# differ are `reserve!`, `payment_state`, `cart_for` and `settlements_for` —
# booking_id against reservation_id, a room-night against a scooter-minute),
# `spec/wire_arguments_spec.rb` 8/7 (T-145; the odd one is each demo's own
# fixture DAY), `script/pow_flow.rb` 7/6, `script/prove_broker_boot.rb` 9/4 (T-147 split four
# per-operator units out of `with_broker` so the other 54 lines could be held),
# `app/operations/wire_arguments.rb` 4/3 (T-120's original path),
# `app/operations/request_kyc_operation.rb` 5/3, `script/register_flow.rb` 4/4,
# `app/services/prove_trust.rb` 3/2, `app/models/booking.rb` 3/1,
# `app/models/cart_mandate.rb` 3/2 and `app/models/user.rb` 3/0 (T-146 — all
# nine of those units are class macros, and every one of them was invisible
# until the handle learned to read a class body),
# `app/controllers/home_controller.rb` 3/1 and
# `app/operations/operation_result.rb` 1/0. That last one holds nothing today:
# its single shared unit is a same-name-different-body declaration. What it
# buys is DISCOVERY over seven copies of shipped code, which is where the next
# method quietly shared by the fleet — the K-792 promotion signal — has to show
# up.
#
# LEFT ALONE: `script/isolation_flow.rb` (8 shared names over seven copies, 4
# identical) and `script/schema_flow.rb` (3/2). Their `get_json` has six and
# three distinct bodies respectively — the same helper written once per demo,
# which is what makes a driver unportable between two of them.
#
# `script/redteam_suite.rb` USED TO LEAD THIS PARAGRAPH with 25 shared names and
# a six-bodied `get_json` of its own. It no longer does: its HTTP driver, result
# ledger, printed vocabulary and exit contract are `Kiosk::Redteam::Wire` and
# `Kiosk::Redteam::Battery`, so `def bearer` and `def record` occur zero times
# across those seven files and `def get_json` once. It stays out of
# FRAGMENT_PATHS for a narrower reason now, stated in FRAGMENT_EXCLUDED below.
#
# THE PATH THE HANDLE COULD NOT REACH, AND WHAT IT COST (T-146).
# `app/models/cart_mandate.rb` has three copies and, until 2026-08-31, ZERO
# units — because what they share is a `scope :referencing, ->`, a `has_many`
# and a `self.table_name`, and FRAGMENT_START matched a `def` or a constant.
# The path was left OUT of FRAGMENT_PATHS and the only record of that was this
# paragraph, which is the defect: an exclusion nothing checks reads exactly
# like coverage. Both halves are closed. The handle now takes its start points
# from the PARSE TREE as well (see class_macro_starts below), which found TEN
# shared units the `def`-or-constant handle had never seen — three in
# cart_mandate.rb, two each in booking.rb, user.rb and home_controller.rb, one
# in request_kyc_operation.rb — and moved the fleet-wide count from 114/72 to
# 124/76. And every `:per_demo` path is now either in FRAGMENT_PATHS or named
# in FRAGMENT_EXCLUDED with a reason, with a path that shares NOTHING reported
# by name rather than counted as a file this rule holds.
#
# A hundred declarations with a hundred reasoned `why:` lines is still a manifest
# nobody would read, and a manifest nobody reads is how «declared» stops meaning
# «reasoned about». The criterion above is what keeps this at 88 entries rather
# than 124, and it is a judgement about VALUE — not a budget.
FRAGMENT_PATHS = %w[
  app/controllers/home_controller.rb
  app/models/booking.rb
  app/models/cart_mandate.rb
  app/models/user.rb
  app/operations/operation_result.rb
  app/operations/request_kyc_operation.rb
  app/operations/wire_arguments.rb
  app/services/prove_trust.rb
  spec/wire_arguments_spec.rb
  script/binding_flow.rb
  script/pay_window.rb
  script/pow_flow.rb
  script/prove_broker_boot.rb
  script/register_flow.rb
].freeze

# AND THE PATHS THAT ARE NOT IN IT, DECLARED (T-146).
#
# THIS LIST EXISTS BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE WAS THE DEFECT. `app/models/cart_mandate.rb`
# was left out of FRAGMENT_PATHS because the handle could not see a `scope`, and
# the only record of that was a paragraph in this file's header — so a path
# contributing NOTHING looked exactly like a path that had been checked. Every
# `:per_demo` entry in COPIES is now either in FRAGMENT_PATHS or named here with
# a reason, and the check below fails on a path in neither, on an entry for a
# path that is not `:per_demo`, on an entry that is also in FRAGMENT_PATHS, and
# on a blank reason. An undeclared exclusion is the thing this rule is about.
FRAGMENT_EXCLUDED = {
  "script/redteam_suite.rb" =>
    "THE HARNESS IS NO LONGER IN THESE FILES, SO THE REASON THIS PATH IS OUT HAS CHANGED — and " \
    "the old one is recorded here because it was this manifest's largest single entry. It read " \
    "«25 unit names shared across seven copies, `get_json` alone has SIX distinct bodies», which " \
    "was the two-harness split: the same helper written six ways, so a beat could not be moved " \
    "between two suites without a rewrite. That is gone. MEASURED at head with `command grep -c` " \
    "over these seven files: `def bearer` and `def record` occur ZERO times, `def get_json` and " \
    "`def post_json` once each — tudu's two-line delegators onto its Devise session, which needs " \
    "the human's cookie jar. The HTTP driver, the result ledger, the printed vocabulary and the " \
    "exit contract are `Kiosk::Redteam::Wire` and `Kiosk::Redteam::Battery`, held by that gem's " \
    "own suite rather than by a copy comparison. WHAT KEEPS THE PATH OUT NOW is narrower and " \
    "durable: what these files still share by NAME is the `Scenario` subclass vocabulary — " \
    "`initialize`, `call`, `refused`, `check`, `pay_with_ref` — over beats that attack different " \
    "verbs, so the bodies genuinely differ and always will. Discovery is all-or-nothing per " \
    "path, so taking it would mean declaring twenty settled `:per_demo` variants to hold a " \
    "handful of units, which is a manifest nobody reads for a guarantee the gem already gives.",
  "script/isolation_flow.rb" =>
    "Same argument as redteam_suite.rb USED to be, and this one is still live: 8 shared names " \
    "over seven copies, 4 identical, and its `get_json` still carries the six-body split the " \
    "red-team files have now shed.",
  "script/schema_flow.rb" =>
    "Same argument again, smallest of the three: 3 shared names over seven copies, 2 identical, " \
    "`get_json` with three distinct bodies. THE OTHER HALF OF THIS PATH IS NO LONGER UNHELD, and " \
    "not by the fragment rule: three of the seven copies (atablefor, philslist, tudu) are " \
    "CODE-IDENTICAL WHOLE FILES at 37 normalised lines each, which the `:per_demo` entry's own " \
    "words did not admit, and they are now declared `:lockstep` in that entry's `agree:` map and " \
    "held there (K-1262). This exclusion therefore costs the FOUR remaining copies' shared units, " \
    "not seven.",
}.freeze

# The demo sets and the reasons several fragment entries share, stated once so N
# copies of a sentence cannot drift apart — the same device as KYC_SHIPS and
# PROVE_SKELETON above.
BINDING_DEMOS  = %w[atablefor philslist stylish].freeze
DEVICE_GRANT   = %w[philslist stylish].freeze
PAY_WINDOW     = %w[hoteling skooti].freeze
SPEC_HARNESS   = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling].freeze

DRIVER_ENV =
  "The driver's half of a contract whose other half is already held: `demo.rake` invokes every " \
  "flow with `SERVER_URL=… KIOSK_ISSUER=…` on the command line, and the flow-runner rule above " \
  "keeps that invocation in lockstep. Here the copies must `fetch` the same NAME with NO default. " \
  "A copy that renames the variable, or that grows a fallback, stops failing loudly on a mis-wired " \
  "task and quietly drives whatever happens to be listening on that port instead."

DEVISE_DELEGATION =
  "One line of the façade these drivers put over {Kiosk::UserIdentityProviders::DeviseSession}, " \
  "the client end of the Devise adapter, shipped by kiosk-user-idp-devise (T-066). The class " \
  "being one class is worth nothing if a driver stops routing through it: these delegations are " \
  "the only " \
  "place the human's cookie jar, CSRF read and sign-in POST are reached from, so a copy that " \
  "inlines its own Net::HTTP call here is precisely the drift the file-level rule cannot see."

WIRE_PAIR =
  "One of the HTTP helpers the driver opens with, answering the `[status, parsed_body]` pair every " \
  "assertion below it reads. On its own it is four lines; what makes it worth holding is that the " \
  "pair must MEAN the same thing in each copy — a one-sided edit here does not break the demo it " \
  "lands in, it changes what a sibling demo's assertions are testing."

FRAGMENTS = {
  "app/operations/wire_arguments.rb#MAX_INT4" => {
    rule:  :code,
    ships: %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling],
    why:   "PostgreSQL `integer`, 2_147_483_647 — the width of the column each demo's guard " \
           "refuses on behalf of (`bookings.party_size`/`restaurant_tables.capacity`, " \
           "`order_items.qty`/`orders.total_cents`, `properties.stars`/`bookings.total_cents`). " \
           "It is the same NUMBER for the same REASON in all three, and K-968's rule — the bound " \
           "is the column's and not a policy — is what makes that a shared fact rather than three " \
           "coincidences. hoteling's copy is the newest (T-125) and folded in the " \
           "`MAX_TOTAL_CENTS` it used to carry under its own name; holding the three in lockstep " \
           "is what stops the next demo inventing a fourth spelling of int4.",
  },
  "app/operations/wire_arguments.rb#ISO_DATE" => {
    rule:  :code,
    ships: %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling],
    why:   "The anchored `YYYY-MM-DD` a date argument must be spelled as, and the only spelling any "\
           "of the three accepts (ADR-0029, K-1406). It is one RULE rather than three coincidences: "\
           "Phil's ruling is that a date on this wire is ISO 8601 and nothing else, so a copy that "\
           "widened on its own would not be a local relaxation, it would be one origin answering a "\
           "different protocol than the two beside it and than the specification. That is exactly "\
           "the one-sided edit this rule exists for, and it is the edit somebody makes to be "\
           "helpful. skooti takes no wire date at all, which is why it is not here.",
  },
  "app/operations/wire_arguments.rb#iso_date" => {
    rule:  :code,
    ships: %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling],
    why:   "The parse behind {ISO_DATE} (ADR-0029, K-1406). `Date.iso8601` runs BEHIND the pattern "\
           "and not instead of it, because ISO 8601 is a FAMILY -- a basic `20260901`, a datetime, "\
           "an ISO week date and an ordinal date all parse through it -- while what the pattern "\
           "still cannot answer is whether a well-shaped value names a real day (`2026-02-30`). "\
